window.__saMergeCourse({id:'kpk-class9-physics', board:'kp', title:'Physics', urdu:'طبیعیات', class:'Class 9', icon:'⚛️', desc:'KP Board Class 9 Physics — full syllabus with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch1', title:'Physical Quantities and Measurement', urdu:'طبعی مقداریں اور پیمائش', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch1-l1', title:'Base and Derived Quantities', html:'<h3>Base and Derived Quantities</h3><p>A <strong>physical quantity</strong> is anything that can be measured, such as length, mass or time. The quantities that do not depend on any other quantity are called <strong>base quantities</strong>. There are seven base quantities in the SI system.</p><table><tr><th>Base quantity</th><th>SI unit</th><th>Symbol</th></tr><tr><td>Length</td><td>metre</td><td>m</td></tr><tr><td>Mass</td><td>kilogram</td><td>kg</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>second</td><td>s</td></tr><tr><td>Electric current</td><td>ampere</td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>Temperature</td><td>kelvin</td><td>K</td></tr><tr><td>Amount of substance</td><td>mole</td><td>mol</td></tr><tr><td>Luminous intensity</td><td>candela</td><td>cd</td></tr></table><p><strong>Derived quantities</strong> are made by combining base quantities. Examples: area (m²), volume (m³), speed (m/s), force — the <strong>newton</strong> (kg·m/s²), and pressure — the <strong>pascal</strong> (N/m²).</p><blockquote>Common exam mistake: mass is a base quantity, but <strong>weight is derived</strong> — it is a force (W = mg) measured in newtons.</blockquote>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch1-l2', title:'Prefixes, Scientific Notation and Significant Figures', html:'<h3>Prefixes and Scientific Notation</h3><p>Very large and very small numbers are written with <strong>prefixes</strong> or in <strong>scientific notation</strong> (a × 10ⁿ).</p><ul><li>giga (G) = 10⁹, mega (M) = 10⁶, kilo (k) = 10³</li><li>milli (m) = 10⁻³, micro (µ) = 10⁻⁶, nano (n) = 10⁻⁹</li></ul><p>Example: 375 km = 375 × 10³ m = 3.75 × 10⁵ m.</p><h3>Significant Figures</h3><ul><li>All non-zero digits are significant: 3.75 has 3.</li><li>Zeros <em>between</em> non-zero digits count: 2007 has 4.</li><li>Leading zeros never count: 0.00520 has 3.</li><li>A final zero after a decimal point counts: 2.50 has 3.</li></ul><p>Your answer should never have more significant figures than the least precise measurement used.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch1-l3', title:'Measuring Instruments and Least Count', html:'<h3>Least Count</h3><p>The <strong>least count</strong> is the smallest value an instrument can measure — one main-scale division divided by the number of vernier (or circular) divisions.</p><ul><li><strong>Vernier callipers:</strong> least count = 0.01 cm = 0.1 mm. Used for small lengths, inner and outer diameters, and depths.</li><li><strong>Screw gauge (micrometer):</strong> least count = 0.01 mm. Used for very small thicknesses like wire diameter.</li><li><strong>Stopwatch:</strong> mechanical ≈ 0.1 s; digital ≈ 0.01 s.</li></ul><p>Reading = main scale reading + (vernier coincidence × least count).</p><h3>Zero Error</h3><p>If the zero of the moving scale does not match the zero of the main scale when the jaws are closed, the instrument has a <strong>zero error</strong>. Subtract a positive zero error from the reading; add a negative one.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'How many base quantities are there in the SI system?', options:['5','6','7','9'], answer:2, explain:'There are exactly seven base quantities: length, mass, time, current, temperature, amount of substance and luminous intensity.'},
{q:'The SI unit of mass is:', options:['gram','pound','kilogram','tonne'], answer:2, explain:'The SI base unit of mass is the kilogram (kg); the gram is a derived everyday unit.'},
{q:'Which of these is a derived quantity?', options:['Length','Time','Force','Mass'], answer:2, explain:'Force = mass × acceleration (kg·m/s²), so it is derived from base quantities; the others are base quantities.'},
{q:'The prefix “micro” stands for:', options:['10⁻³','10⁻⁶','10⁻⁹','10⁶'], answer:1, explain:'micro (µ) = 10⁻⁶; milli = 10⁻³ and nano = 10⁻⁹.'},
{q:'The number 0.00520 has how many significant figures?', options:['2','3','4','5'], answer:1, explain:'Leading zeros never count; the significant digits are 5, 2 and the final 0 — three in total.'},
{q:'The least count of vernier callipers is:', options:['1 mm','0.1 mm','0.01 mm','1 cm'], answer:1, explain:'One main-scale division (1 mm) divided by 10 vernier divisions gives a least count of 0.1 mm = 0.01 cm.'},
{q:'A screw gauge is best used to measure:', options:['The length of a classroom','The diameter of a thin wire','The depth of a beaker','The mass of a stone'], answer:1, explain:'A screw gauge (least count 0.01 mm) measures very small lengths such as wire diameter.'},
{q:'A positive zero error in vernier callipers must be:', options:['Added to the reading','Subtracted from the reading','Multiplied by 10','Ignored'], answer:1, explain:'Observed reading = true reading + zero error, so a positive zero error is subtracted to get the true value.'},
{q:'Which instrument measures time most precisely?', options:['Wall clock','Mechanical stopwatch','Digital stopwatch','Sundial'], answer:2, explain:'A digital stopwatch (least count 0.01 s) is more precise than a mechanical stopwatch (0.1 s).'},
{q:'Weight is measured in newtons because it is:', options:['A base quantity','A derived quantity (a force)','A dimensionless number','A prefix'], answer:1, explain:'Weight W = mg is a force, hence derived from mass, length and time — its SI unit is the newton.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch2', title:'Kinematics', urdu:'حرکیات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch2-l1', title:'Rest, Motion and Its Types', html:'<h3>Rest and Motion</h3><p>A body is at <strong>rest</strong> if it does not change its position with time relative to its surroundings; it is in <strong>motion</strong> if it does. Rest and motion are relative — a passenger in a moving bus is at rest relative to the bus but in motion relative to the road.</p><h3>Types of Motion</h3><ul><li><strong>Translatory motion:</strong> the whole body moves from one place to another (a car on a road, a ball rolling). It may be <em>rectilinear</em> (straight line) or <em>curvilinear</em> (curved path).</li><li><strong>Rotatory motion:</strong> the body spins about a fixed axis (a ceiling fan, the Earth).</li><li><strong>Vibratory motion:</strong> to-and-fro motion about a mean position (a swing, a tuning fork).</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch2-l2', title:'Scalars, Vectors, Speed and Velocity', html:'<h3>Scalar vs Vector</h3><p><strong>Scalars</strong> have magnitude only (distance, speed, time, mass). <strong>Vectors</strong> have magnitude and direction (displacement, velocity, force, acceleration).</p><ul><li><strong>Distance:</strong> total path length (scalar). <strong>Displacement:</strong> shortest straight-line change of position (vector).</li><li><strong>Speed</strong> = distance/time (scalar). <strong>Velocity</strong> = displacement/time (vector); SI unit m/s.</li><li><strong>Acceleration</strong> = change of velocity/time, a = (vf − vi)/t; SI unit m/s².</li></ul><p>Uniform velocity means covering equal displacements in equal time intervals — so acceleration is zero.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch2-l3', title:'Equations of Motion and Graphs', html:'<h3>The Three Equations of Motion</h3><p>For motion with uniform acceleration:</p><blockquote>vf = vi + at<br>S = vit + ½at²<br>2aS = vf² − vi²</blockquote><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> A car starts from rest (vi = 0) and accelerates at 2 m/s² for 5 s. vf = 0 + 2×5 = 10 m/s. Distance S = 0 + ½×2×25 = 25 m.</p><h3>Graphs</h3><ul><li><strong>Distance–time graph:</strong> slope = speed; a straight line means uniform speed.</li><li><strong>Speed–time graph:</strong> slope = acceleration; <strong>area under the graph = distance</strong> covered.</li></ul>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'A ceiling fan turning about its fixed axis shows:', options:['Translatory motion','Rotatory motion','Vibratory motion','Random motion'], answer:1, explain:'Rotation about a fixed axis is rotatory motion.'},
{q:'Which of the following is a vector quantity?', options:['Distance','Speed','Displacement','Time'], answer:2, explain:'Displacement has both magnitude and direction, so it is a vector; the others are scalars.'},
{q:'The SI unit of acceleration is:', options:['m/s','m/s²','km/h','N'], answer:1, explain:'Acceleration = change of velocity ÷ time, giving (m/s)/s = m/s².'},
{q:'A body moving with uniform velocity has:', options:['Increasing acceleration','Zero acceleration','Decreasing speed','Changing direction'], answer:1, explain:'Uniform velocity means no change of velocity, so acceleration is zero.'},
{q:'The first equation of motion is:', options:['S = vit + ½at²','vf = vi + at','2aS = vf² − vi²','v = S/t'], answer:1, explain:'vf = vi + at relates final velocity to initial velocity, acceleration and time.'},
{q:'A car starts from rest and accelerates at 3 m/s² for 4 s. Its final velocity is:', options:['7 m/s','12 m/s','10 m/s','24 m/s'], answer:1, explain:'vf = vi + at = 0 + 3×4 = 12 m/s.'},
{q:'In a speed–time graph, the area under the curve gives:', options:['Acceleration','Distance covered','Force','Momentum'], answer:1, explain:'Area = speed × time = distance covered.'},
{q:'The slope of a distance–time graph gives:', options:['Acceleration','Speed','Force','Displacement'], answer:1, explain:'Slope = distance ÷ time = speed.'},
{q:'If a body returns to its starting point, its displacement is:', options:['Equal to distance','Twice the distance','Zero','Maximum'], answer:2, explain:'Displacement is the net change of position; returning to the start gives zero displacement.'},
{q:'Free fall near the Earth’s surface has acceleration of about:', options:['1.6 m/s²','9.8 m/s²','25 m/s²','0 m/s²'], answer:1, explain:'Objects in free fall accelerate at g ≈ 9.8 m/s² (often taken as 10 m/s² in numericals).'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch3', title:'Dynamics', urdu:'قوتوں کا علم', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch3-l1', title:'Force, Inertia and Newton’s First Law', html:'<h3>Force</h3><p>A <strong>force</strong> is a push or a pull that changes (or tends to change) the state of rest or uniform motion of a body. Its SI unit is the <strong>newton (N)</strong>.</p><h3>Inertia and the First Law</h3><p><strong>Inertia</strong> is the tendency of a body to resist any change in its state of rest or motion. Newton’s first law: <em>a body continues in its state of rest or uniform motion unless acted upon by an unbalanced external force</em>.</p><p>Example: passengers lurch forward when a bus stops suddenly — their bodies tend to keep moving.</p><h3>Momentum</h3><p><strong>Momentum</strong> p = mv (unit kg·m/s). A heavier or faster body has more momentum and is harder to stop.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch3-l2', title:'Newton’s Second and Third Laws', html:'<h3>Second Law: F = ma</h3><p>The acceleration produced in a body is directly proportional to the net force and inversely proportional to its mass:</p><blockquote>F = ma</blockquote><p>So 1 N is the force that gives 1 kg an acceleration of 1 m/s². <strong>Worked example:</strong> F = 5 kg × 2 m/s² = 10 N.</p><h3>Weight vs Mass</h3><p><strong>Mass</strong> (kg) is the amount of matter — constant everywhere. <strong>Weight</strong> W = mg (N) is the gravitational force on that mass — it changes with g. On the Moon (g ≈ 1.6 m/s²) your weight is about one-sixth of Earth’s.</p><h3>Third Law</h3><p><em>For every action there is an equal and opposite reaction.</em> The two forces act on <strong>different</strong> bodies — that is why they do not cancel.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch3-l3', title:'Friction and Circular Motion', html:'<h3>Friction</h3><p><strong>Friction</strong> opposes relative motion between surfaces in contact. Types: <strong>static</strong> (before sliding starts), <strong>sliding/kinetic</strong>, and <strong>rolling</strong> (smallest).</p><p>Friction is useful (walking, brakes, writing) and harmful (wear, heat, wasted energy). It is reduced by lubrication, polishing, ball bearings and streamlining.</p><h3>Circular Motion</h3><p>A body moving in a circle needs a <strong>centripetal force</strong> directed towards the centre:</p><blockquote>Fc = mv²/r</blockquote><p>Examples: a stone whirled on a string, the Moon orbiting the Earth, a car turning a corner. Without centripetal force the body would fly off along the tangent.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'The SI unit of force is:', options:['Joule','Newton','Watt','Pascal'], answer:1, explain:'Force is measured in newtons (N); 1 N = 1 kg·m/s².'},
{q:'Inertia is the tendency of a body to:', options:['Change its speed','Resist change in its state of rest or motion','Fall to the ground','Move in circles'], answer:1, explain:'Inertia resists any change of state — this is Newton’s first law.'},
{q:'A force of 10 N acts on a mass of 2 kg. The acceleration is:', options:['5 m/s²','20 m/s²','8 m/s²','12 m/s²'], answer:0, explain:'a = F/m = 10/2 = 5 m/s².'},
{q:'The momentum of a 4 kg body moving at 3 m/s is:', options:['7 kg·m/s','12 kg·m/s','1.33 kg·m/s','0.75 kg·m/s'], answer:1, explain:'p = mv = 4 × 3 = 12 kg·m/s.'},
{q:'Your mass on the Moon compared to Earth is:', options:['One-sixth','Six times','The same','Zero'], answer:2, explain:'Mass is the amount of matter and does not change; only weight changes with g.'},
{q:'Newton’s third law forces act on:', options:['The same body','Different bodies','Only stationary bodies','Only the Earth'], answer:1, explain:'Action and reaction act on different bodies, so they never cancel each other.'},
{q:'Which type of friction is the smallest?', options:['Static','Sliding','Rolling','Limiting'], answer:2, explain:'Rolling friction is far smaller than sliding or static friction — that is why wheels and ball bearings are used.'},
{q:'Centripetal force always points:', options:['Along the tangent','Away from the centre','Towards the centre of the circle','Upwards'], answer:2, explain:'Centripetal means “centre-seeking”; Fc = mv²/r is directed towards the centre.'},
{q:'Passengers lurch forward when a bus stops suddenly because of:', options:['Friction','Inertia','Gravity','Momentum'], answer:1, explain:'Their bodies tend to continue moving forward — inertia of motion.'},
{q:'One newton is the force that gives 1 kg an acceleration of:', options:['9.8 m/s²','1 m/s²','10 m/s²','100 m/s²'], answer:1, explain:'By definition F = ma, so 1 N = 1 kg × 1 m/s².'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch4', title:'Turning Effect of Forces', urdu:'قوتوں کا گردشی اثر', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch4-l1', title:'Moment of Force and Torque', html:'<h3>Moment of Force</h3><p>The <strong>moment of a force</strong> (turning effect) about a point is:</p><blockquote>Moment = force × perpendicular distance from the pivot</blockquote><p>Unit: newton-metre (N·m). A longer spanner handle gives a bigger moment for the same force — that is why door handles are far from the hinges.</p><h3>Torque</h3><p><strong>Torque</strong> is the moment of a force that causes rotation, τ = rF sinθ. A <strong>couple</strong> is two equal, opposite, parallel forces (like turning a steering wheel) whose resultant force is zero but turning effect is not.</p><h3>Principle of Moments</h3><p>A body is balanced when <em>sum of clockwise moments = sum of anticlockwise moments</em> about the pivot — the idea behind beam balances and see-saws.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch4-l2', title:'Centre of Mass and Centre of Gravity', html:'<h3>Centre of Mass</h3><p>The <strong>centre of mass</strong> is the point where the whole mass of a body appears concentrated. For a uniform rod it is the midpoint; for a uniform circular disc, the geometric centre.</p><h3>Centre of Gravity</h3><p>The <strong>centre of gravity</strong> is the point where the whole <strong>weight</strong> of the body appears to act. Near the Earth’s surface it coincides with the centre of mass.</p><p>It can be found by suspending an irregular lamina from different points — the centre of gravity lies where the plumb lines cross.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch4-l3', title:'Equilibrium and Stability', html:'<h3>Conditions of Equilibrium</h3><p>A body is in equilibrium when:</p><ul><li>ΣF = 0 — the vector sum of all forces is zero (translational equilibrium).</li><li>Στ = 0 — the sum of all torques about any point is zero (rotational equilibrium).</li></ul><h3>States of Equilibrium</h3><ul><li><strong>Stable:</strong> returns to its position when slightly disturbed (a cone on its base).</li><li><strong>Unstable:</strong> topples further when disturbed (a cone on its tip).</li><li><strong>Neutral:</strong> stays in the new position (a ball on a flat table).</li></ul><p><strong>Stability</strong> increases with a lower centre of gravity and a wider base — racing cars are built low and wide for this reason.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'The moment of a force is calculated as:', options:['Force ÷ distance','Force × perpendicular distance from pivot','Force + distance','Force × time'], answer:1, explain:'Moment = F × d, where d is the perpendicular distance from the line of action to the pivot.'},
{q:'The SI unit of torque is:', options:['N/m','N·m','J/s','kg·m'], answer:1, explain:'Torque = force × distance, so its unit is the newton-metre (N·m).'},
{q:'Door handles are placed far from the hinges because:', options:['They look better','A larger distance gives a larger moment for the same force','It reduces friction','It increases the mass'], answer:1, explain:'Moment = F × d; increasing d increases the turning effect without extra force.'},
{q:'A couple consists of:', options:['Two equal forces in the same direction','Two equal, opposite, parallel forces','One force and one mass','Two pivots'], answer:1, explain:'A couple is two equal, opposite, parallel forces whose resultant is zero but torque is not.'},
{q:'The principle of moments states that a balanced body has:', options:['Clockwise moments greater','Anticlockwise moments greater','Clockwise = anticlockwise moments','Zero mass'], answer:2, explain:'For rotational equilibrium the clockwise and anticlockwise moments about the pivot must be equal.'},
{q:'The centre of mass of a uniform metre rod is at:', options:['The 0 cm mark','The 50 cm mark','The 100 cm mark','Outside the rod'], answer:1, explain:'For a uniform body the centre of mass is at its geometric centre — 50 cm for a metre rod.'},
{q:'For translational equilibrium:', options:['ΣF = 0','Στ = 0','ΣF = ma','v = 0'], answer:0, explain:'Translational equilibrium needs zero net force; Στ = 0 is the rotational condition.'},
{q:'A cone standing on its tip is in:', options:['Stable equilibrium','Unstable equilibrium','Neutral equilibrium','No equilibrium'], answer:1, explain:'A slight disturbance makes it topple further — the mark of unstable equilibrium.'},
{q:'Stability of a body increases when its centre of gravity is:', options:['Raised higher','Lowered','Removed','Shifted sideways'], answer:1, explain:'A lower centre of gravity and wider base make a body more stable.'},
{q:'Racing cars are built low and wide mainly to:', options:['Look stylish','Increase stability','Reduce mass','Increase speed directly'], answer:1, explain:'Low centre of gravity plus wide base gives high stability in fast turns.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch5', title:'Gravitation', urdu:'کششِ ثقل', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch5-l1', title:'Newton’s Law of Universal Gravitation', html:'<h3>The Law</h3><p>Every body attracts every other body with a force directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centres:</p><blockquote>F = G·m₁m₂ / r²</blockquote><p>G is the <strong>universal gravitational constant</strong>, G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg², first measured by Cavendish.</p><h3>Mass of the Earth</h3><p>Using g = GM/R² with g = 9.8 m/s² and R = 6.4 × 10⁶ m, the mass of the Earth works out to M ≈ 6.0 × 10²⁴ kg.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch5-l2', title:'Variation of g and Weightlessness', html:'<h3>Variation of g</h3><ul><li><strong>With altitude:</strong> g decreases as you go up, because g = GM/(R+h)².</li><li><strong>With depth:</strong> g decreases going down into a mine, becoming zero at the Earth’s centre.</li><li><strong>On the Moon:</strong> g ≈ 1.6 m/s², about one-sixth of Earth’s — astronauts bounce when they walk.</li></ul><h3>Weightlessness</h3><p>A freely falling body feels <strong>weightless</strong> because there is no support force pushing back on it. Astronauts in orbit are in continuous free fall around the Earth, which is why they float.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch5-l3', title:'Satellites and Orbital Motion', html:'<h3>Orbital Velocity</h3><p>A satellite stays in orbit because gravity supplies the centripetal force. Near the Earth’s surface the required <strong>orbital velocity</strong> is:</p><blockquote>v₀ = √(GM/R) ≈ 7.9 km/s</blockquote><h3>Types of Satellites</h3><ul><li><strong>Geostationary satellites</strong> orbit at ~36,000 km with a 24-hour period, staying fixed over one point — used for TV and communications.</li><li><strong>Polar/low-orbit satellites</strong> pass over the poles for weather and mapping.</li></ul><p>If the launch speed is below 7.9 km/s the satellite falls back; above about 11.2 km/s (escape velocity) it leaves the Earth forever.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'Newton’s law of gravitation: the force between two masses is proportional to:', options:['The sum of the masses','The product of the masses divided by r²','The distance between them','The difference of the masses'], answer:1, explain:'F = Gm₁m₂/r² — product of masses over the square of the distance.'},
{q:'The value of the gravitational constant G is:', options:['9.8 N·m²/kg²','6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg²','1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C','3 × 10⁸ m/s'], answer:1, explain:'G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg², measured by Cavendish.'},
{q:'The mass of the Earth is approximately:', options:['6.0 × 10²⁴ kg','6.0 × 10⁶ kg','9.8 × 10²⁴ kg','1.6 × 10²⁴ kg'], answer:0, explain:'From g = GM/R², the Earth’s mass works out to about 6.0 × 10²⁴ kg.'},
{q:'As altitude increases, the value of g:', options:['Increases','Stays the same','Decreases','Becomes zero at once'], answer:2, explain:'g = GM/(R+h)², so growing h shrinks g.'},
{q:'At the centre of the Earth, g is:', options:['9.8 m/s²','4.9 m/s²','Zero','Maximum'], answer:2, explain:'g falls with depth and becomes zero at the Earth’s centre.'},
{q:'The value of g on the Moon is about:', options:['9.8 m/s²','1.6 m/s²','25 m/s²','0.98 m/s²'], answer:1, explain:'The Moon’s smaller mass gives g ≈ 1.6 m/s², roughly one-sixth of Earth’s.'},
{q:'Astronauts in orbit feel weightless because:', options:['There is no gravity in space','They are in continuous free fall around the Earth','Their mass becomes zero','Space suits cancel weight'], answer:1, explain:'Orbit is continuous free fall; with no support force, they feel weightless.'},
{q:'The orbital velocity of a satellite near the Earth’s surface is about:', options:['1.6 km/s','7.9 km/s','11.2 km/s','3 × 10⁵ km/s'], answer:1, explain:'v₀ = √(GM/R) ≈ 7.9 km/s close to the Earth.'},
{q:'A geostationary satellite has a time period of:', options:['90 minutes','12 hours','24 hours','1 month'], answer:2, explain:'It must match the Earth’s rotation — 24 hours — to stay over one point.'},
{q:'The escape velocity from Earth is about:', options:['7.9 km/s','9.8 km/s','11.2 km/s','16 km/s'], answer:2, explain:'About 11.2 km/s is needed to escape Earth’s gravity completely.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch6', title:'Work and Energy', urdu:'کام اور توانائی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch6-l1', title:'Work and Kinetic Energy', html:'<h3>Work</h3><p><strong>Work</strong> is done when a force moves a body through a distance in the direction of the force:</p><blockquote>W = F × d (unit: joule, J)</blockquote><p>1 joule = work done by 1 N moving a body 1 m. No displacement, no work — holding a heavy bag stationary does zero work in physics.</p><h3>Kinetic Energy</h3><p><strong>Kinetic energy</strong> is the energy of a moving body:</p><blockquote>K.E. = ½mv²</blockquote><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> a 2 kg ball at 10 m/s has K.E. = ½ × 2 × 100 = 100 J. Note: doubling speed quadruples kinetic energy.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch6-l2', title:'Potential Energy and Conservation of Energy', html:'<h3>Potential Energy</h3><p><strong>Potential energy</strong> is stored energy due to position:</p><blockquote>P.E. = mgh</blockquote><p>A 1 kg book on a 2 m shelf stores P.E. = 1 × 10 × 2 = 20 J (using g = 10 m/s²).</p><h3>Forms and Conservation</h3><p>Energy appears as mechanical, heat, light, sound, electrical, chemical and nuclear energy. The <strong>law of conservation of energy</strong>: <em>energy can neither be created nor destroyed; it only changes form.</em> A falling ball converts P.E. into K.E.; at the ground its K.E. equals the P.E. it started with.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch6-l3', title:'Power, Efficiency and Energy Sources', html:'<h3>Power</h3><p><strong>Power</strong> is the rate of doing work:</p><blockquote>P = W/t (unit: watt, W)</blockquote><p>1 W = 1 J/s. Larger units: 1 kW = 1000 W, 1 MW = 10⁶ W, 1 hp ≈ 746 W.</p><h3>Efficiency</h3><blockquote>Efficiency = (useful output ÷ total input) × 100%</blockquote><h3>Energy Sources</h3><p><strong>Renewable:</strong> solar, wind, hydro, biomass, geothermal — they do not run out. <strong>Non-renewable:</strong> coal, oil, natural gas, nuclear fuel — limited and polluting. Pakistan’s Tarbela and Mangla dams generate hydroelectricity.</p><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> a machine does 500 J in 10 s: P = 500/10 = 50 W.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'The SI unit of work is:', options:['Watt','Newton','Joule','Pascal'], answer:2, explain:'Work = force × distance, so 1 J = 1 N × 1 m.'},
{q:'No work is done when:', options:['A box is lifted','A car moves forward','A person holds a heavy bag stationary','A ball rolls downhill'], answer:2, explain:'Work needs displacement in the force’s direction; holding still gives W = 0.'},
{q:'The kinetic energy of a 2 kg body moving at 10 m/s is:', options:['20 J','100 J','200 J','10 J'], answer:1, explain:'K.E. = ½mv² = ½ × 2 × 100 = 100 J.'},
{q:'If the speed of a body is doubled, its kinetic energy becomes:', options:['Double','Four times','Half','Eight times'], answer:1, explain:'K.E. ∝ v², so doubling v multiplies K.E. by 2² = 4.'},
{q:'Potential energy of a 5 kg mass raised 4 m (g = 10 m/s²) is:', options:['20 J','200 J','50 J','9 J'], answer:1, explain:'P.E. = mgh = 5 × 10 × 4 = 200 J.'},
{q:'The law of conservation of energy states that energy:', options:['Can be created','Can be destroyed','Only changes form','Decreases with time'], answer:2, explain:'Energy is neither created nor destroyed, only converted from one form to another.'},
{q:'Power is defined as:', options:['Force × distance','Work ÷ time','Mass × velocity','Energy × time'], answer:1, explain:'Power is the rate of doing work: P = W/t, measured in watts.'},
{q:'A machine does 600 J of work in 20 s. Its power is:', options:['30 W','12000 W','620 W','580 W'], answer:0, explain:'P = W/t = 600/20 = 30 W.'},
{q:'Efficiency of a machine is calculated as:', options:['Input ÷ output','(Useful output ÷ input) × 100%','Output + input','Output − input'], answer:1, explain:'Efficiency compares useful energy out with total energy in, as a percentage.'},
{q:'Which of these is a renewable energy source?', options:['Coal','Natural gas','Solar energy','Petrol'], answer:2, explain:'Solar energy is renewed by nature; coal, gas and petrol are finite fossil fuels.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch7', title:'Properties of Matter', urdu:'مادے کی خصوصیات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch7-l1', title:'States of Matter and Density', html:'<h3>Kinetic Molecular Model</h3><p>Matter is made of tiny particles in constant motion. In <strong>solids</strong> particles vibrate in fixed positions (rigid, fixed volume); in <strong>liquids</strong> they slide past each other (fixed volume, no fixed shape); in <strong>gases</strong> they move freely (no fixed volume or shape).</p><h3>Density</h3><blockquote>Density = mass ÷ volume (unit: kg/m³)</blockquote><p>Water’s density is 1000 kg/m³. A body floats if its density is less than the fluid’s — ships float because their <em>average</em> density (steel + air inside) is less than water’s.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch7-l2', title:'Pressure and Pascal’s Law', html:'<h3>Pressure</h3><blockquote>Pressure = force ÷ area (unit: pascal, Pa)</blockquote><p>1 Pa = 1 N/m². Sharp knives cut because a small area gives huge pressure for the same force.</p><h3>Pascal’s Law</h3><p><em>Pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted equally in all directions.</em> A <strong>hydraulic press</strong> uses this: a small force on a small piston lifts a heavy load on a large piston (car lifts, brakes).</p><h3>Atmospheric Pressure</h3><p>The air’s weight gives atmospheric pressure ≈ 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa, supporting a 76 cm column of mercury in a barometer. It is why we can drink with a straw and why suction cups stick.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch7-l3', title:'Elasticity and Hooke’s Law', html:'<h3>Deformation</h3><p><strong>Elasticity</strong> is the ability of a body to return to its original shape after a deforming force is removed (a spring); <strong>plasticity</strong> is permanent deformation (wet clay).</p><h3>Hooke’s Law</h3><p><em>Within the elastic limit, extension is directly proportional to the stretching force:</em></p><blockquote>F = k × Δl</blockquote><p>where k is the spring constant (N/m).</p><h3>Stress, Strain and Young’s Modulus</h3><p><strong>Stress</strong> = force ÷ area; <strong>strain</strong> = extension ÷ original length (no unit). <strong>Young’s modulus</strong> Y = stress ÷ strain measures stiffness — steel’s Y is far larger than rubber’s.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'According to the kinetic molecular model, particles in a solid:', options:['Move freely','Vibrate about fixed positions','Slide past each other','Do not move at all'], answer:1, explain:'Solid particles are locked in a lattice and only vibrate about fixed positions.'},
{q:'The SI unit of density is:', options:['kg/m²','kg/m³','N/m²','g/cm'], answer:1, explain:'Density = mass ÷ volume, so kg/m³.'},
{q:'A ship floats because:', options:['Steel is light','Its average density is less than water’s','Water has no density','Gravity is zero at sea'], answer:1, explain:'The hollow hull makes average density (steel + air) less than 1000 kg/m³, so it floats.'},
{q:'Pressure is defined as:', options:['Force × area','Force ÷ area','Mass × volume','Work ÷ time'], answer:1, explain:'P = F/A; a small area gives large pressure.'},
{q:'Pascal’s law states that pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is:', options:['Lost as heat','Transmitted equally in all directions','Doubled','Reduced to zero'], answer:1, explain:'This equal transmission is the working principle of hydraulic lifts and brakes.'},
{q:'Atmospheric pressure at sea level is about:', options:['1.01 × 10⁵ Pa','1.01 × 10³ Pa','76 Pa','1013 N'], answer:0, explain:'Standard atmospheric pressure ≈ 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa, equal to 76 cm of mercury.'},
{q:'Hooke’s law is valid:', options:['Always','Only within the elastic limit','Only for gases','Only at high temperature'], answer:1, explain:'Beyond the elastic limit the extension is no longer proportional to force.'},
{q:'In F = kΔl, the constant k is called the:', options:['Spring constant','Gas constant','Gravitational constant','Planck constant'], answer:0, explain:'k (N/m) measures the stiffness of the spring.'},
{q:'Strain is defined as:', options:['Force ÷ area','Extension ÷ original length','Mass ÷ volume','Stress × area'], answer:1, explain:'Strain = Δl/l and has no unit since it is length ÷ length.'},
{q:'Young’s modulus measures a material’s:', options:['Density','Stiffness','Colour','Melting point'], answer:1, explain:'Y = stress ÷ strain; a larger Y means a stiffer material (steel > rubber).'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch8', title:'Thermal Properties of Matter', urdu:'مادے کی حرارتی خصوصیات', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch8-l1', title:'Temperature, Heat and Thermometers', html:'<h3>Heat vs Temperature</h3><p><strong>Heat</strong> is the total thermal energy flowing from a hot body to a cold one (unit: joule). <strong>Temperature</strong> measures how hot a body is — the average kinetic energy of its particles.</p><h3>Scales</h3><ul><li><strong>Celsius (°C)</strong>, <strong>Kelvin (K)</strong> — the SI scale, <strong>Fahrenheit (°F)</strong>.</li><li>K = °C + 273; °F = (9/5)°C + 32.</li><li><strong>Absolute zero</strong> = 0 K = −273°C — the lowest possible temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> 25°C = 298 K; 25°C = (9/5)(25) + 32 = 77°F.</p><p>Liquid-in-glass thermometers use mercury or alcohol; clinical thermometers have a kink to hold the reading.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch8-l2', title:'Specific Heat Capacity and Latent Heat', html:'<h3>Specific Heat Capacity</h3><p><strong>Specific heat capacity (c)</strong> is the heat needed to raise 1 kg of a substance by 1 K:</p><blockquote>Q = mcΔT</blockquote><p>Water’s c = 4200 J/kg·K — very high, which is why water is used in car radiators and why coastal areas have mild climates.</p><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> heating 2 kg of water by 10 K needs Q = 2 × 4200 × 10 = 84,000 J.</p><h3>Latent Heat</h3><p>Heat absorbed or released during a change of state without temperature change is <strong>latent heat</strong>. Latent heat of fusion of ice = 3.36 × 10⁵ J/kg; of vaporisation of water = 2.26 × 10⁶ J/kg.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-phy-ch8-l3', title:'Thermal Expansion and Transfer of Heat', html:'<h3>Thermal Expansion</h3><p>Most substances expand when heated. <strong>Linear expansion:</strong> ΔL = αL₀ΔT. Railway tracks have gaps and bridges have expansion joints for this reason. <strong>Volume expansion</strong> of a solid is about three times its linear expansion (β = 3α).</p><p><strong>Anomalous expansion of water:</strong> water contracts when heated from 0°C to 4°C — ice is less dense than water, so it floats.</p><h3>Transfer of Heat</h3><ul><li><strong>Conduction:</strong> through solids by particle vibration — metals are good conductors.</li><li><strong>Convection:</strong> by bulk movement of fluids — sea breezes, room heaters.</li><li><strong>Radiation:</strong> by electromagnetic waves, needs no medium — heat from the Sun. Black surfaces absorb and radiate best.</li></ul>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'Heat is best described as:', options:['Average kinetic energy of particles','Energy flowing from hot to cold body','A temperature scale','A type of thermometer'], answer:1, explain:'Heat is energy in transit due to a temperature difference; temperature measures average particle kinetic energy.'},
{q:'25°C in kelvin is:', options:['248 K','273 K','298 K','77 K'], answer:2, explain:'K = °C + 273 = 25 + 273 = 298 K.'},
{q:'Absolute zero equals:', options:['0°C','−273°C','−100°C','273°F'], answer:1, explain:'0 K = −273°C, the temperature at which particle motion is minimal.'},
{q:'The specific heat capacity of water is:', options:['420 J/kg·K','4200 J/kg·K','1000 J/kg·K','336000 J/kg·K'], answer:1, explain:'Water’s unusually high c = 4200 J/kg·K makes it an excellent coolant.'},
{q:'Heat needed to raise 2 kg of water by 10 K is:', options:['8400 J','84000 J','4200 J','21000 J'], answer:1, explain:'Q = mcΔT = 2 × 4200 × 10 = 84,000 J.'},
{q:'Latent heat is absorbed or released:', options:['With a rise in temperature','Without any change of temperature','Only above 100°C','Only by metals'], answer:1, explain:'During melting or boiling the temperature stays constant while latent heat changes the state.'},
{q:'Railway tracks are laid with small gaps to allow for:', options:['Contraction at night','Thermal expansion in heat','Easier repair','Noise reduction'], answer:1, explain:'Steel expands when hot (ΔL = αL₀ΔT); gaps prevent buckling.'},
{q:'Ice floats on water because:', options:['Ice is hotter','Ice is less dense than water','Water has no density','Of surface tension'], answer:1, explain:'Water’s anomalous expansion makes ice (0.917 g/cm³) less dense than liquid water.'},
{q:'Heat from the Sun reaches the Earth by:', options:['Conduction','Convection','Radiation','Evaporation'], answer:2, explain:'Radiation travels as electromagnetic waves and needs no medium.'},
{q:'Which surface is the best absorber and radiator of heat?', options:['Shiny white','Black and dull','Polished silver','Transparent glass'], answer:1, explain:'Dull black surfaces absorb and emit thermal radiation most efficiently.'}
]}
]});

