window.__saMergeCourse({id:'kpk-class9-english', board:'kp', title:'English', urdu:'انگریزی', class:'Class 9', icon:'📖', desc:'KP Board Class 9 English — all 12 units with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch1', title:'Hazrat Muhammad the Model of Tolerance', urdu:'حضرت محمد ﷺ — رواداری کا نمونہ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch1-l1', title:'The Lesson: A Life of Tolerance', html:'<h3>Theme: Tolerance</h3><p>This lesson presents Hazrat Muhammad (peace be upon him) as the perfect model of <strong>tolerance</strong> — patience with opponents, forgiveness of enemies and kindness to all.</p><h3>Examples from the Lesson</h3><ul><li>At the <strong>conquest of Makkah</strong>, when his persecutors were at his mercy, he announced a general amnesty: <em>“No blame shall rest on you this day.”</em></li><li>He forgave the old woman who used to throw rubbish on his path, and visited her when she fell ill.</li><li>At <strong>Taif</strong>, though pelted with stones, he prayed for the people’s guidance instead of their punishment.</li><li>The <strong>Charter of Madina</strong> guaranteed rights to Jews and other communities.</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch1-l2', title:'Vocabulary and Key Words', html:'<h3>Words to Master</h3><ul><li><strong>Tolerance</strong> — willingness to accept others’ views and faults.</li><li><strong>Amnesty</strong> — a general pardon.</li><li><strong>Persecution</strong> — cruel treatment for beliefs.</li><li><strong>Forbearance</strong> — patient self-control.</li><li><strong>Compassion</strong> — deep sympathy for others’ suffering.</li></ul><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> learn the contextual meanings of underlined words in the lesson — vocabulary MCQs come straight from the text.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch1-l3', title:'Grammar Focus: Tenses', html:'<h3>Indefinite Tenses</h3><ul><li><strong>Present indefinite:</strong> Subject + verb(s/es) — “He forgives his enemies.”</li><li><strong>Past indefinite:</strong> Subject + verb 2nd form — “He forgave his enemies.”</li><li><strong>Future indefinite:</strong> Subject + will/shall + verb — “He will forgive.”</li></ul><p>Use present indefinite for habits and universal truths (“The sun rises in the east”); past indefinite for completed past actions.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'The central theme of the lesson is:', options:['Bravery','Tolerance','Wealth','Politics'], answer:1, explain:'The lesson shows the Holy Prophet (PBUH) as the model of tolerance.'},
{q:'At the conquest of Makkah, the Holy Prophet (PBUH) announced:', options:['Severe punishment','General amnesty','Heavy taxes','Exile of all'], answer:1, explain:'He forgave his former persecutors: “No blame shall rest on you this day.”'},
{q:'When the old woman who threw rubbish fell ill, the Holy Prophet (PBUH):', options:['Ignored her','Visited her','Punished her','Left the city'], answer:1, explain:'His visit to the sick woman shows practical compassion.'},
{q:'At Taif, the Holy Prophet (PBUH) responded to cruelty with:', options:['Revenge','Prayer for the people’s guidance','A boycott','Silence only'], answer:1, explain:'Though injured, he prayed for Taif’s people rather than cursing them.'},
{q:'The word “amnesty” means:', options:['War','General pardon','Tax','Treaty'], answer:1, explain:'Amnesty is an official pardon for offences.'},
{q:'“He forgives his enemies.” is in:', options:['Past indefinite','Present indefinite','Future indefinite','Present continuous'], answer:1, explain:'Subject + first form of verb = present indefinite (habitual action).'},
{q:'“They will follow the truth.” is in:', options:['Past indefinite','Present indefinite','Future indefinite','Past continuous'], answer:2, explain:'will + first form of verb = future indefinite.'},
{q:'Tolerance means:', options:['Fighting opponents','Accepting others’ views and faults patiently','Ignoring everyone','Seeking revenge'], answer:1, explain:'Tolerance is patient acceptance of differences.'},
{q:'The Charter of Madina guaranteed rights to:', options:['Muslims only','Jews and other communities too','Traders only','Soldiers only'], answer:1, explain:'It was a plural society pact protecting all communities of Madina.'},
{q:'“Forbearance” is closest in meaning to:', options:['Anger','Patient self-control','Weakness','Pride'], answer:1, explain:'Forbearance means restraining oneself with patience.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch2', title:'Iqbal’s Message to Youth', urdu:'اقبال کا نوجوانوں کے لیے پیغام', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch2-l1', title:'The Lesson: Iqbal’s Call to the Young', html:'<h3>Theme: Patriotism and Selfhood</h3><p>Allama Iqbal called the youth to <strong>khudi</strong> (selfhood/self-reliance) — believe in yourself, act with faith and build your destiny instead of begging from fate.</p><ul><li><strong>The Shaheen (eagle)</strong> is Iqbal’s symbol for the ideal youth: high-flying, self-reliant, never feeding on others’ leftovers.</li><li>His famous verse: <em>“Khudi ko kar buland itna, ke har taqdeer se pehle / Khuda bande se khud pooche, bata teri raza kya hai.”</em></li><li>Message: combine <strong>faith, knowledge and action</strong>; the youth are the architects of Pakistan’s future.</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch2-l2', title:'Vocabulary and Key Words', html:'<h3>Words to Master</h3><ul><li><strong>Khudi</strong> — selfhood, self-reliance.</li><li><strong>Shaheen</strong> — eagle; Iqbal’s symbol of noble youth.</li><li><strong>Destiny</strong> — what fate has in store.</li><li><strong>Vision</strong> — a clear idea of the future.</li><li><strong>Endeavour</strong> — hard, serious effort.</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch2-l3', title:'Grammar Focus: Active and Passive Voice', html:'<h3>Changing Voice</h3><p><strong>Active:</strong> the subject does the action (“Iqbal inspired the youth.”). <strong>Passive:</strong> the subject receives it (“The youth were inspired by Iqbal.”).</p><ul><li>Present indefinite: “She writes a letter.” → “A letter is written by her.”</li><li>Past indefinite: “He built a school.” → “A school was built by him.”</li><li>Future indefinite: “They will elect him.” → “He will be elected by them.”</li></ul>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'Iqbal’s symbol for the ideal youth is the:', options:['Dove','Shaheen (eagle)','Sparrow','Peacock'], answer:1, explain:'The high-flying, self-reliant eagle represents Iqbal’s ideal young person.'},
{q:'“Khudi” means:', options:['Wealth','Selfhood and self-reliance','Fame','Prayer only'], answer:1, explain:'Khudi is Iqbal’s core concept of the self’s dignity and power.'},
{q:'Iqbal’s message to youth combines:', options:['Faith, knowledge and action','Wealth, fame and comfort','Songs and dances','Silence and rest'], answer:0, explain:'The lesson stresses faith, learning and practical effort together.'},
{q:'“The youth were inspired by Iqbal.” is in:', options:['Active voice','Passive voice','Indirect speech','Imperative mood'], answer:1, explain:'The subject receives the action — passive voice.'},
{q:'Passive of “She writes a letter.” is:', options:['A letter was written by her','A letter is written by her','A letter will be written by her','She is written by a letter'], answer:1, explain:'Present indefinite passive uses is/am/are + third form.'},
{q:'Passive of “He built a school.” is:', options:['A school is built by him','A school was built by him','A school has been built by him','He was built by a school'], answer:1, explain:'Past indefinite passive uses was/were + third form.'},
{q:'The word “endeavour” means:', options:['Laziness','Hard, serious effort','Luck','Delay'], answer:1, explain:'Endeavour is earnest striving towards a goal.'},
{q:'Iqbal wanted the youth to be:', options:['Dependent on others','Self-reliant architects of the future','Idle dreamers','Followers of fashion'], answer:1, explain:'Khudi demands self-belief and nation-building action.'},
{q:'“Destiny” is closest in meaning to:', options:['Past','Fate','Wealth','Dream'], answer:1, explain:'Destiny is what the future holds.'},
{q:'In “Khudi ko kar buland itna…”, the poet asks man to raise his self so high that:', options:['He becomes proud','God Himself asks his will before decreeing fate','He fears nothing','He rules others'], answer:1, explain:'The verse says God will ask the servant his wish before writing destiny.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch3', title:'Quaid — A Great Leader', urdu:'قائد — ایک عظیم رہنما', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch3-l1', title:'The Lesson: Jinnah the Leader', html:'<h3>Theme: Role Model</h3><p>Quaid-e-Azam Muhammad Ali Jinnah united the Muslims of the subcontinent and won Pakistan through constitutional struggle.</p><ul><li>On <strong>7 August 1947</strong> his silver aircraft landed at <strong>Mauripur, Karachi</strong>, to a rousing welcome.</li><li>His <strong>Fourteen Points</strong> (1929) safeguarded Muslim rights.</li><li>Qualities: <strong>honesty, determination, courage, hard work</strong> and unshakeable faith in the cause.</li><li>His motto for the nation: <strong>Unity, Faith, Discipline</strong>.</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch3-l2', title:'Vocabulary and Key Words', html:'<h3>Words to Master</h3><ul><li><strong>Determination</strong> — firm decision to do something.</li><li><strong>Integrity</strong> — honesty and strong moral principles.</li><li><strong>Struggle</strong> — a hard fight for a cause.</li><li><strong>Constitutional</strong> — according to law and constitution.</li><li><strong>Leadership</strong> — the ability to guide people.</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch3-l3', title:'Grammar Focus: Parts of Speech', html:'<h3>The Eight Parts of Speech</h3><ul><li><strong>Noun:</strong> names a person, place or thing (“Jinnah”, “Pakistan”).</li><li><strong>Pronoun:</strong> replaces a noun (“he”, “they”).</li><li><strong>Verb:</strong> action or state (“led”, “was”).</li><li><strong>Adjective:</strong> describes a noun (“great leader”).</li><li><strong>Adverb:</strong> describes a verb (“bravely”).</li><li><strong>Preposition, Conjunction, Interjection</strong> join or exclaim.</li></ul>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'The Quaid’s aircraft landed at Mauripur, Karachi on:', options:['14 August 1947','7 August 1947','23 March 1940','25 December 1946'], answer:1, explain:'He arrived on 7 August 1947 to a historic welcome.'},
{q:'The Quaid’s Fourteen Points were presented in:', options:['1929','1940','1947','1916'], answer:0, explain:'The Fourteen Points (1929) defended Muslim political rights.'},
{q:'The Quaid’s motto for the nation was:', options:['Faith, Unity, Discipline','Unity, Faith, Discipline','Work, Worship, Win','Peace, Love, Hope'], answer:1, explain:'Unity, Faith, Discipline — in that order.'},
{q:'The lesson presents the Quaid mainly as a:', options:['Poet','Role model of leadership','Scientist','Trader'], answer:1, explain:'Theme of the lesson: a great leader as role model.'},
{q:'“Integrity” means:', options:['Wealth','Honesty and strong moral principles','Strength','Speed'], answer:1, explain:'Integrity is doing right even when unseen.'},
{q:'In “Jinnah was a great leader”, the word “great” is a/an:', options:['Noun','Adjective','Adverb','Verb'], answer:1, explain:'It describes the noun “leader”.'},
{q:'In “He led bravely”, the word “bravely” is a/an:', options:['Adjective','Adverb','Noun','Pronoun'], answer:1, explain:'It describes the verb “led”.'},
{q:'Pakistan came into being on:', options:['14 August 1947','15 August 1947','23 March 1940','7 August 1947'], answer:0, explain:'Independence was achieved on 14 August 1947.'},
{q:'The Quaid won Pakistan mainly through:', options:['Armed revolt','Constitutional and political struggle','Foreign help','Luck'], answer:1, explain:'His weapon was law, argument and unbreakable resolve.'},
{q:'“Determination” is closest in meaning to:', options:['Doubt','Firm resolve','Fear','Delay'], answer:1, explain:'Determination is a fixed purpose to achieve something.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch4', title:'The Daffodils (Poem)', urdu:'ڈیفوڈلز (نظم)', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch4-l1', title:'The Poet and the Poem', html:'<h3>William Wordsworth</h3><p><strong>William Wordsworth</strong> (1770–1850), the great English nature poet, wrote “The Daffodils” (also called “I Wandered Lonely as a Cloud”) after seeing daffodils with his sister Dorothy at Ullswater lake.</p><h3>Summary</h3><p>Wandering lonely as a cloud, the poet suddenly sees a <strong>crowd of golden daffodils</strong> beside the lake, beneath the trees, <strong>fluttering and dancing in the breeze</strong> — “continuous as the stars that shine”. Their <strong>jocund company</strong> fills his heart with pleasure, and later, in lonely moments, their memory flashes upon his <strong>inward eye</strong> — “the bliss of solitude”.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch4-l2', title:'Stanza-wise Explanation', html:'<h3>Key Lines</h3><ul><li><strong>Stanza 1:</strong> “I wandered lonely as a cloud” — the poet compares himself to a drifting cloud; then “all at once I saw a crowd” of daffodils.</li><li><strong>Stanza 2:</strong> “Ten thousand saw I at a glance” — countless flowers, outdoing the sparkling waves “in glee”.</li><li><strong>Stanza 3:</strong> “A poet could not but be gay / In such a jocund company” — no one can stay sad among such joy.</li><li><strong>Stanza 4:</strong> lying on his couch, the memory returns: “my heart with pleasure fills, / And dances with the daffodils.”</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch4-l3', title:'Figures of Speech and Vocabulary', html:'<h3>Figures of Speech</h3><ul><li><strong>Simile:</strong> “lonely <em>as a cloud</em>”, “continuous <em>as the stars</em>”.</li><li><strong>Personification:</strong> daffodils “dancing”, “tossing their heads in sprightly dance”.</li><li><strong>Hyperbole:</strong> “ten thousand” daffodils.</li></ul><h3>Vocabulary</h3><ul><li><strong>Jocund</strong> — happy; <strong>glee</strong> — joy; <strong>at a glance</strong> — in one look; <strong>gay</strong> — cheerful; <strong>bliss of solitude</strong> — the joy of peaceful loneliness.</li></ul>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'The poem “The Daffodils” was written by:', options:['John Keats','William Wordsworth','P. B. Shelley','Robert Frost'], answer:1, explain:'Wordsworth, the nature poet, wrote it after the Ullswater walk.'},
{q:'“I wandered lonely as a cloud” contains a:', options:['Metaphor','Simile','Hyperbole','Pun'], answer:1, explain:'Comparison with “as” makes it a simile.'},
{q:'The daffodils are described as “continuous as the stars” — this is a:', options:['Simile','Metaphor','Personification','Alliteration'], answer:0, explain:'“As” comparison between flowers and stars.'},
{q:'“Jocund company” means:', options:['Sad gathering','Happy company','Large crowd','Quiet group'], answer:1, explain:'Jocund means cheerful and merry.'},
{q:'“Ten thousand saw I at a glance” shows:', options:['Exact counting','Hyperbole','Simile','Irony'], answer:1, explain:'Deliberate exaggeration for effect.'},
{q:'The “inward eye” refers to:', options:['A disease','Memory and imagination','A telescope','The soul’s window literally'], answer:1, explain:'In solitude the poet re-sees the scene in memory.'},
{q:'The daffodils grew:', options:['On a mountain','Beside the lake, beneath the trees','In a garden','In a desert'], answer:1, explain:'The poem places them along the lake under trees.'},
{q:'“And dances with the daffodils” shows the poet’s heart is:', options:['Sad','Filled with pleasure','Afraid','Bored'], answer:1, explain:'The memory fills his heart with pleasure.'},
{q:'Daffodils “tossing their heads in sprightly dance” is:', options:['Simile','Personification','Hyperbole','Oxymoron'], answer:1, explain:'Human action (dancing) is given to flowers.'},
{q:'The poem’s main theme is:', options:['War','The lasting joy nature gives the mind','City life','Science'], answer:1, explain:'Nature’s beauty comforts the poet long after the moment.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch5', title:'The Madina Charter', urdu:'میثاقِ مدینہ', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch5-l1', title:'The Lesson: The First Written Constitution', html:'<h3>Background</h3><p>After the <strong>Hijrah (622 AD)</strong>, Hazrat Muhammad (PBUH) united Madina’s tribes — Muslims (Muhajirin and Ansar), Jews and pagans — through a written pact, the <strong>Mithaq-e-Madina</strong>, history’s first written constitution.</p><h3>Main Principles</h3><ul><li>All communities formed <strong>one ummah</strong> (one political nation).</li><li><strong>Freedom of religion</strong> for Jews and others.</li><li><strong>Mutual defence:</strong> all would defend Madina together.</li><li><strong>Justice for all:</strong> disputes to be settled by the Prophet (PBUH); no tribe would shelter criminals.</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch5-l2', title:'Vocabulary and Key Words', html:'<h3>Words to Master</h3><ul><li><strong>Charter</strong> — a written statement of rights.</li><li><strong>Constitution</strong> — the basic law of a state.</li><li><strong>Coexistence</strong> — living together peacefully.</li><li><strong>Covenant</strong> — a solemn agreement.</li><li><strong>Harmony</strong> — peaceful agreement.</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch5-l3', title:'Grammar Focus: Articles', html:'<h3>A, An, The</h3><ul><li><strong>A/an:</strong> indefinite — “a charter”, “an agreement” (an before vowel sounds).</li><li><strong>The:</strong> definite — “the Madina Charter” (a specific, known thing).</li></ul><p>Rules: “an honest man” (silent h → vowel sound); “a university” (yoo → consonant sound). No article before plural general nouns: “Charters protect rights.”</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'The Madina Charter was written after the:', options:['Conquest of Makkah','Hijrah in 622 AD','Battle of Badr','Treaty of Hudaybiyyah'], answer:1, explain:'It followed the migration to Madina in 622 AD.'},
{q:'The Madina Charter is famous as:', options:['A trade deal','The first written constitution','A war plan','A poem'], answer:1, explain:'It is regarded as history’s first written constitution.'},
{q:'Under the Charter, the Jews of Madina were given:', options:['No rights','Freedom of religion and protection','Exile orders','Heavy taxes only'], answer:1, explain:'The Charter guaranteed their faith and security as one ummah.'},
{q:'The Charter bound all communities to:', options:['Fight each other','Defend Madina together','Pay tribute to Makkah','Leave the city'], answer:1, explain:'Mutual defence of the city was a core clause.'},
{q:'Disputes under the Charter were to be settled by:', options:['Tribal chiefs','The Holy Prophet (PBUH)','A council of Jews','The strongest tribe'], answer:1, explain:'The Prophet (PBUH) was the final authority in disputes.'},
{q:'“Coexistence” means:', options:['Living together peacefully','Fighting together','Trading only','Ruling others'], answer:0, explain:'Co-existence is peaceful living side by side.'},
{q:'Choose the correct article: “___ honest man”', options:['A','An','The','No article'], answer:1, explain:'“Honest” starts with a vowel sound (silent h) → an.'},
{q:'Choose the correct article: “___ Madina Charter”', options:['A','An','The','None'], answer:2, explain:'A specific, famous document → the definite article.'},
{q:'A “covenant” is a:', options:['Casual promise','Solemn agreement','Fight','Journey'], answer:1, explain:'A covenant is a formal, binding pact.'},
{q:'The Charter made the people of Madina:', options:['Separate nations','One ummah (political nation)','Slaves','Soldiers only'], answer:1, explain:'Despite different faiths, they were one political community.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch6', title:'Nasiruddin', urdu:'نصیر الدین', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch6-l1', title:'The Lesson: Tales of Wit', html:'<h3>Theme: Humour</h3><p><strong>Mullah Nasiruddin</strong> is the beloved wise-fool of Eastern folk tales. His stories are famous for their unique style and everyday situations that make us laugh <em>and</em> think.</p><h3>Anecdotes from the Lesson</h3><ul><li><strong>Jalal’s introduction:</strong> Nasiruddin introduces his friend Jalal in such a funny way that the humour lies in the introduction itself.</li><li><strong>The thieves:</strong> finding robbers in his house, Nasiruddin hides in a cupboard and says he was too <strong>ashamed to face them — there was nothing in his house worth stealing</strong>.</li><li><strong>The donkey:</strong> a man who trusts a donkey’s word over a man’s does not deserve a loan.</li><li><strong>The gardener:</strong> caught stealing vegetables, Nasiruddin claims the <strong>wind blew him in</strong> and he was just wondering how the vegetables got into his sack.</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch6-l2', title:'Character Sketch and Message', html:'<h3>Character of Nasiruddin</h3><p>Nasiruddin is <strong>witty, quick-thinking and humorous</strong>. He escapes trouble with clever excuses, laughs at human folly and teaches wisdom through laughter. His humour is gentle — he makes people smile, never weep.</p><h3>Message</h3><p>Laughter is a gift; wit can solve problems that force cannot. The stories also mirror real life — such characters exist in every society.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch6-l3', title:'Grammar Focus: Adjectives', html:'<h3>Kinds of Adjectives</h3><ul><li><strong>Quality:</strong> “a <em>funny</em> story”.</li><li><strong>Quantity:</strong> “<em>some</em> vegetables”.</li><li><strong>Number:</strong> “<em>two</em> thieves”.</li><li><strong>Demonstrative:</strong> “<em>that</em> man”.</li><li><strong>Possessive:</strong> “<em>his</em> sack”.</li></ul><h3>Degrees</h3><p>Positive (clever) → Comparative (cleverer) → Superlative (cleverest). <strong>Order:</strong> opinion, size, age, colour, origin — “a <em>funny little old</em> man”.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'Nasiruddin’s stories are famous for their:', options:['Sad endings','Unique style and everyday situations','Long poems','Historical dates'], answer:1, explain:'The lesson says they deal with day-to-day life in a unique, funny style.'},
{q:'When thieves entered his house, Nasiruddin:', options:['Fought them','Hid in a cupboard, ashamed his house had nothing worth stealing','Called the police','Ran away'], answer:1, explain:'His witty shame disarmed the thieves.'},
{q:'The point of laughter in the donkey story is that a man who prefers a donkey’s word to a man’s:', options:['Is wise','Does not deserve to be lent anything','Should be rewarded','Is a scholar'], answer:1, explain:'The lesson’s own explanation of the joke.'},
{q:'To the gardener, Nasiruddin explained that:', options:['He bought the vegetables','The wind blew him in and he wondered how vegetables got into his sack','He was the owner','He was dreaming'], answer:1, explain:'His classic absurd excuse.'},
{q:'Nasiruddin’s character is best described as:', options:['Cruel and dull','Witty, humorous and quick-thinking','Lazy and rude','Proud and silent'], answer:1, explain:'Evidence from every anecdote shows gentle wit.'},
{q:'The theme of the lesson is:', options:['Justice','Humour','War','Science'], answer:1, explain:'The textbook’s theme for this unit is humour.'},
{q:'In “a funny story”, the adjective is of:', options:['Quantity','Quality','Number','Demonstrative'], answer:1, explain:'“Funny” describes the quality of the story.'},
{q:'The superlative of “clever” is:', options:['Cleverer','Cleverest','More clever','Most cleverer'], answer:1, explain:'One-syllable adjectives add -est.'},
{q:'In “that man”, the word “that” is a:', options:['Possessive adjective','Demonstrative adjective','Adjective of number','Adverb'], answer:1, explain:'It points out which man.'},
{q:'The correct adjective order is:', options:['Old funny little man','Funny little old man','Little old funny man','Man funny little old'], answer:1, explain:'Opinion → size → age: funny, little, old.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch7', title:'The Two Bargains', urdu:'دو سودے', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch7-l1', title:'The Lesson: What is True Justice?', html:'<h3>Theme: Justice</h3><p>This lesson is a story about a king whose court confuses <strong>hasty punishment with justice</strong>. When a complaint is brought, the king orders executions without proper inquiry — even sentencing a goldsmith who was only following the king’s own orders.</p><h3>The Message</h3><p>True justice needs <strong>patience, evidence and fairness</strong>. Power without wisdom becomes tyranny; the lesson warns that a ruler who punishes blindly ends by punishing the innocent — and finally himself.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch7-l2', title:'Reading the Story Critically', html:'<h3>Key Moments to Remember</h3><ul><li>The <strong>goldsmith’s excuse</strong>: he was merely following the king’s orders to finish the queen’s ornaments — yet the king will not listen.</li><li>The <strong>hangman’s problem</strong>: the noose does not fit the goldsmith, so the king orders it tried on <em>everyone</em> — justice reduced to a lottery.</li><li>The <strong>disciple</strong> at last fits the noose — the innocent pay for the ruler’s folly.</li></ul><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> questions ask for the <em>moral</em> and your <em>opinion</em> — always support answers with events from the story.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch7-l3', title:'Vocabulary and Key Words', html:'<h3>Words to Master</h3><ul><li><strong>Justice</strong> — fair treatment according to law.</li><li><strong>Bargain</strong> — an agreement or deal.</li><li><strong>Mercy</strong> — kindness that stops punishment.</li><li><strong>Tyranny</strong> — cruel, unjust rule.</li><li><strong>Innocent</strong> — not guilty.</li><li><strong>Excuse</strong> — a reason given to defend oneself.</li></ul>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'The theme of “The Two Bargains” is:', options:['Humour','Justice','Technology','Nature'], answer:1, explain:'The textbook’s theme for this unit is justice.'},
{q:'The king in the story is shown as:', options:['Wise and patient','Hasty and unjust in punishment','Kind and forgiving','Silent and weak'], answer:1, explain:'He orders hangings without proper inquiry.'},
{q:'The goldsmith’s excuse was that he:', options:['Was innocent of everything','Was only following the king’s orders','Had bribed the guards','Was asleep'], answer:1, explain:'He had been ordered to set aside work for the queen’s ornaments.'},
{q:'The hangman could not hang the goldsmith because:', options:['The king pardoned him','The noose did not fit his neck','He escaped','It was night'], answer:1, explain:'The noose was too loose — leading to the absurd order to try it on everyone.'},
{q:'The lesson teaches that true justice requires:', options:['Speed','Patience, evidence and fairness','Harsh punishment','Silence'], answer:1, explain:'Hasty punishment of the innocent is tyranny, not justice.'},
{q:'“Mercy” means:', options:['Cruelty','Kindness that stops punishment','Wealth','Power'], answer:1, explain:'Mercy tempers justice with compassion.'},
{q:'A “bargain” is:', options:['A fight','An agreement or deal','A journey','A gift'], answer:1, explain:'A bargain is a negotiated deal.'},
{q:'The disciple in the story represents:', options:['The guilty','The innocent victim of unjust rule','The king’s friend','A thief'], answer:1, explain:'The noose finally fits the innocent disciple.'},
{q:'“Tyranny” is closest in meaning to:', options:['Fair rule','Cruel, unjust rule','Democracy','Kindness'], answer:1, explain:'Tyranny is oppressive misuse of power.'},
{q:'The main fault of the king’s “justice” is that it:', options:['Is too slow','Punishes without proper inquiry','Frees everyone','Costs too much'], answer:1, explain:'Punishment without evidence is the story’s warning.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch8', title:'Hope is the Thing with Feathers (Poem)', urdu:'امید — پروں والی چیز (نظم)', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch8-l1', title:'The Poet and the Poem', html:'<h3>Emily Dickinson</h3><p><strong>Emily Dickinson</strong> (1830–1886), the great American poet, compares <strong>hope</strong> to a bird in this beloved poem.</p><h3>Summary</h3><p>Hope is “the thing with feathers” that <strong>perches in the soul</strong> and <strong>sings without words, never stopping</strong>. It sings sweetest <strong>in the gale</strong> (hard times); only the fiercest storm could frighten it. The poet has heard it in the <strong>chillest land</strong> and on the <strong>strangest sea</strong> — yet it <strong>never asked a crumb</strong> of her: hope gives freely, asking nothing back.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch8-l2', title:'Stanza-wise Explanation', html:'<h3>Stanza 1</h3><p>Hope = a bird living in the soul, singing an endless wordless tune — optimism never fully leaves us.</p><h3>Stanza 2</h3><p>“Sweetest in the gale is heard” — hope shines brightest in suffering; “sore must be the storm / That could abash the little bird” — almost nothing can crush it.</p><h3>Stanza 3</h3><p>Hope survives the coldest, strangest places, yet “never… asked a crumb of me” — it is a free, selfless gift.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch8-l3', title:'Figures of Speech and Vocabulary', html:'<h3>Figures of Speech</h3><ul><li><strong>Metaphor:</strong> hope <em>is</em> the thing with feathers (no “like”/“as”).</li><li><strong>Personification:</strong> hope “perches”, “sings”, is “abashed”.</li><li><strong>Imagery:</strong> gale, chillest land, strangest sea.</li></ul><h3>Vocabulary</h3><ul><li><strong>Perch</strong> — to sit/rest (as a bird); <strong>gale</strong> — a strong wind; <strong>abash</strong> — to embarrass or dishearten; <strong>crumb</strong> — a tiny piece.</li></ul>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'“Hope is the Thing with Feathers” was written by:', options:['Emily Brontë','Emily Dickinson','Elizabeth Barrett Browning','Sylvia Plath'], answer:1, explain:'The American poet Emily Dickinson (1830–1886).'},
{q:'In the poem, hope is compared to a:', options:['Candle','Bird','Tree','River'], answer:1, explain:'“The thing with feathers” — a bird.'},
{q:'This comparison (without “like” or “as”) is a:', options:['Simile','Metaphor','Hyperbole','Pun'], answer:1, explain:'Direct identification makes it a metaphor.'},
{q:'Hope “perches in the soul” — “perches” means:', options:['Flies away','Sits and rests','Sings loudly','Dies'], answer:1, explain:'To perch is to settle as a bird does.'},
{q:'Hope sings sweetest:', options:['In calm weather','In the gale','At dawn','In summer'], answer:1, explain:'Hard times make hope’s song sweetest.'},
{q:'“Sore must be the storm / That could abash the little bird” means:', options:['Storms kill birds','Almost nothing can crush hope','Birds fear storms','Hope is weak'], answer:1, explain:'Only the fiercest suffering could silence hope.'},
{q:'“Never… asked a crumb of me” shows hope is:', options:['Hungry','Selfless and free','Expensive','Rare'], answer:1, explain:'Hope gives without demanding anything back.'},
{q:'“And sings the tune without the words” suggests hope:', options:['Is silent','Needs no explanation — it is felt','Is a real bird','Cannot sing'], answer:1, explain:'Hope is an inner feeling beyond words.'},
{q:'Giving human qualities to hope is:', options:['Simile','Personification','Alliteration','Irony'], answer:1, explain:'Hope perches, sings and is abashed like a person.'},
{q:'The poem’s message is:', options:['Avoid birds','Hope survives all hardships and costs nothing','Travel by sea','Fear storms'], answer:1, explain:'Hope endures everywhere and asks nothing in return.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch9', title:'The Fantastic Shoemaker', urdu:'حیرت انگیز موچی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch9-l1', title:'The Lesson: Dignity of Labour', html:'<h3>Theme: Dignity of Labour</h3><p>This lesson celebrates the <strong>dignity of honest manual work</strong>. The shoemaker takes pride in his craft: every shoe he mends is made with skill, care and honesty.</p><h3>The Message</h3><ul><li><strong>No work is low</strong> — a cobbler’s honest labour deserves as much respect as any profession.</li><li><strong>Skill and honesty</strong> win customers’ trust and a good name.</li><li>Work done with love becomes <strong>“fantastic”</strong> — extraordinary — because of the worker’s spirit, not the job’s title.</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch9-l2', title:'Reading for Meaning', html:'<h3>What Makes Him “Fantastic”?</h3><p>Not magic, but <strong>attitude</strong>: the shoemaker works hard, charges fairly, treats every customer kindly and never cheats. His workshop may be small, but his <strong>character is large</strong>.</p><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> comprehension questions ask <em>why</em> he is called fantastic — answer with his qualities: hard work, skill, honesty and pride in labour.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch9-l3', title:'Vocabulary and Key Words', html:'<h3>Words to Master</h3><ul><li><strong>Dignity</strong> — the quality of being worthy of respect.</li><li><strong>Labour</strong> — hard physical or mental work.</li><li><strong>Craft</strong> — a skilled trade.</li><li><strong>Honest</strong> — truthful and fair.</li><li><strong>Fantastic</strong> — extraordinary, wonderful.</li><li><strong>Livelihood</strong> — a means of earning a living.</li></ul>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'The theme of “The Fantastic Shoemaker” is:', options:['Magic','Dignity of labour','War','Travel'], answer:1, explain:'The textbook’s theme for this unit is dignity of labour.'},
{q:'The shoemaker is called “fantastic” because of his:', options:['Magic powers','Skill, honesty and pride in his work','Wealth','Large shop'], answer:1, explain:'His extraordinary spirit, not magic, makes him fantastic.'},
{q:'The lesson teaches that:', options:['Manual work is low','No honest work is low','Only office jobs matter','Shoemaking is easy'], answer:1, explain:'All honest labour deserves respect.'},
{q:'“Dignity” means:', options:['Pride without reason','The quality of being worthy of respect','High birth','Richness'], answer:1, explain:'Dignity is earned worth, not given status.'},
{q:'A “livelihood” is:', options:['A life story','A means of earning a living','A long journey','A hobby'], answer:1, explain:'One’s livelihood is how one earns bread.'},
{q:'The shoemaker wins customers mainly through:', options:['Low prices only','Honest work and fair dealing','Advertisements','Luck'], answer:1, explain:'Skill plus honesty builds lasting trust.'},
{q:'“Craft” in the lesson means:', options:['A boat','A skilled trade','Cunning','Art class'], answer:1, explain:'Shoemaking is his craft — his skilled trade.'},
{q:'Which quality is NOT shown by the shoemaker?', options:['Honesty','Hard work','Cheating customers','Pride in work'], answer:2, explain:'He never cheats; honesty is central to his character.'},
{q:'The lesson’s message for students is to:', options:['Avoid manual work','Respect all honest work','Seek only rich jobs','Work without pay'], answer:1, explain:'Dignity of labour applies to every honest profession.'},
{q:'“Fantastic” is closest in meaning to:', options:['Ordinary','Extraordinary','Frightening','Imaginary'], answer:1, explain:'Fantastic = remarkably good, wonderful.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch10', title:'Technology in Everyday Life', urdu:'روزمرہ زندگی میں ٹیکنالوجی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch10-l1', title:'The Lesson: How Technology Shapes Us', html:'<h3>Theme: Technology</h3><p>Technology — the application of science — touches every part of modern life:</p><ul><li><strong>Communication:</strong> mobile phones and the internet connect the world instantly.</li><li><strong>Medicine:</strong> vaccines, scanners and surgery save millions of lives.</li><li><strong>Transport:</strong> cars, trains and aeroplanes shrink distances.</li><li><strong>Education:</strong> online learning brings classrooms to every home.</li><li><strong>Home:</strong> washing machines, refrigerators and fans ease daily work.</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch10-l2', title:'Boon and Bane', html:'<h3>Advantages</h3><p>Faster work, better health, wider knowledge, comfort and connection across distances.</p><h3>Disadvantages</h3><p>Pollution, <strong>addiction to screens</strong>, less physical activity, job losses to machines, and misuse (e.g. spread of false news).</p><h3>The Balanced View</h3><p>Technology is a <strong>tool</strong> — its value depends on how wisely humans use it.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch10-l3', title:'Vocabulary and Key Words', html:'<h3>Words to Master</h3><ul><li><strong>Technology</strong> — applying science to practical life.</li><li><strong>Innovation</strong> — a new idea or method.</li><li><strong>Device</strong> — a machine made for a purpose.</li><li><strong>Addiction</strong> — harmful dependence.</li><li><strong>Efficient</strong> — working well without waste.</li><li><strong>Pollution</strong> — harmful contamination of the environment.</li></ul>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'Technology means:', options:['Pure science','Applying science to practical life','Magic','Nature'], answer:1, explain:'Technology puts scientific knowledge to practical use.'},
{q:'Which is a benefit of technology in medicine?', options:['More diseases','Vaccines and scanners saving lives','Costly travel','Noise'], answer:1, explain:'Medical technology vastly improves health care.'},
{q:'A disadvantage of technology mentioned is:', options:['Faster communication','Screen addiction and less activity','Better schools','Cleaner air'], answer:1, explain:'Misuse and overuse harm health and society.'},
{q:'“Innovation” means:', options:['Copying old ideas','A new idea or method','Breaking machines','Ignoring science'], answer:1, explain:'Innovation is novel, useful change.'},
{q:'The balanced view of technology is that it is:', options:['Always good','Always bad','A tool — good or bad as its use','Useless'], answer:2, explain:'Wisdom in use decides its value.'},
{q:'“Efficient” means:', options:['Slow','Working well without waste','Expensive','Large'], answer:1, explain:'Efficiency is maximum output for minimum input.'},
{q:'Online learning is an example of technology in:', options:['Transport','Education','Medicine','Farming'], answer:1, explain:'The internet brings classrooms home.'},
{q:'Spread of false news online is a:', options:['Benefit','Misuse of technology','Invention','Device'], answer:1, explain:'It shows the harmful side of connectivity.'},
{q:'A “device” is:', options:['A wish','A machine made for a purpose','A disease','A plan'], answer:1, explain:'Devices are purpose-built tools or machines.'},
{q:'Pollution caused by factories is a:', options:['Benefit of technology','Harmful effect of technology','Type of device','Innovation'], answer:1, explain:'Industrial pollution is technology’s environmental cost.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch11', title:'Safety First', urdu:'حفاظت سب سے پہلے', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch11-l1', title:'The Lesson: Safety on the Road', html:'<h3>Theme: Safety</h3><p>Most road accidents are caused by <strong>carelessness</strong> — over-speeding, ignoring signals and not wearing helmets or seatbelts. <strong>Safety first</strong> means protecting life before everything else.</p><h3>Traffic Rules to Remember</h3><ul><li>Obey <strong>traffic signals</strong>: red = stop, yellow = get ready, green = go.</li><li>Use <strong>zebra crossings</strong>; look left, right, then left again.</li><li>Wear a <strong>helmet</strong> on a motorcycle and a <strong>seatbelt</strong> in a car.</li><li>Never use a mobile phone while driving; never drink and drive.</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch11-l2', title:'Safety at Home and School', html:'<h3>Everyday Precautions</h3><ul><li>Keep medicines and chemicals away from children.</li><li>Handle <strong>electricity</strong> with dry hands; never touch broken wires.</li><li>Know basic <strong>first aid</strong>: clean a wound, stop bleeding with pressure, call for help.</li><li>In case of <strong>fire</strong>: raise the alarm, leave calmly, never use lifts.</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch11-l3', title:'Vocabulary and Key Words', html:'<h3>Words to Master</h3><ul><li><strong>Safety</strong> — freedom from danger.</li><li><strong>Precaution</strong> — care taken beforehand.</li><li><strong>Accident</strong> — an unexpected harmful event.</li><li><strong>Signal</strong> — a sign giving information.</li><li><strong>First aid</strong> — immediate help before a doctor comes.</li><li><strong>Helmet</strong> — protective headgear.</li></ul>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'The main cause of road accidents is:', options:['Bad luck','Carelessness and rule-breaking','Good roads','Slow traffic'], answer:1, explain:'Over-speeding, ignoring signals and distraction cause most crashes.'},
{q:'A red traffic signal means:', options:['Go','Get ready','Stop','Turn left'], answer:2, explain:'Red = stop; yellow = prepare; green = go.'},
{q:'Pedestrians should cross at:', options:['Anywhere','Zebra crossings','Roundabouts','Bridges only'], answer:1, explain:'Zebra crossings mark the safe place to cross.'},
{q:'A motorcyclist must wear a:', options:['Cap','Helmet','Scarf','Goggles only'], answer:1, explain:'Helmets protect the head in a crash.'},
{q:'“First aid” is:', options:['Hospital surgery','Immediate help before a doctor arrives','A medicine','An ambulance'], answer:1, explain:'First aid is the first response at the scene.'},
{q:'A “precaution” is:', options:['Care taken beforehand','Care taken afterwards','Ignoring danger','A punishment'], answer:0, explain:'Pre-caution = caution in advance.'},
{q:'In case of fire, you should:', options:['Use the lift','Raise the alarm and leave calmly','Hide under a bed','Open all windows'], answer:1, explain:'Calm, quick exit saves lives; lifts are death traps in fires.'},
{q:'“Safety first” means:', options:['Speed first','Protecting life before everything else','Money first','Fun first'], answer:1, explain:'No journey or task is worth a life.'},
{q:'Using a mobile while driving is dangerous because it:', options:['Saves time','Distracts attention from the road','Charges the battery','Is illegal only'], answer:1, explain:'Distraction delays reaction and causes crashes.'},
{q:'Medicines at home should be kept:', options:['On the table','Away from children’s reach','In the kitchen','Near toys'], answer:1, explain:'Child-proof storage prevents poisoning.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch12', title:'The Old Woman (Poem)', urdu:'بوڑھی عورت (نظم)', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch12-l1', title:'The Poet and the Poem', html:'<h3>Joseph Campbell</h3><p><strong>Joseph Campbell</strong> (1879–1944), the Irish poet, finds <strong>beauty in old age</strong> in this short, simple poem of three stanzas.</p><h3>Summary</h3><p>The poet looks at an old woman’s face and finds it beautiful. He compares it to <strong>a white candle in a holy place</strong> (a symbol of light and guidance), to the <strong>winter’s sun</strong> (soft, warm radiance), and to <strong>water under a ruined mill</strong> (still, deep, thoughtful). Her aged face glows with the <strong>wisdom of a long life</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch12-l2', title:'The Three Comparisons', html:'<h3>Stanza by Stanza</h3><ul><li><strong>White candle in a holy place:</strong> like a candle that gives light to worshippers, the old woman’s experience <strong>guides others</strong>.</li><li><strong>Winter’s sun:</strong> not harsh but gentle and warm — the soft beauty of age.</li><li><strong>Water under a ruined mill:</strong> calm and deep — her <strong>still, thoughtful mind</strong>.</li></ul><p>All three are <strong>similes</strong> — comparisons using “like” or “as”.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c9-eng-ch12-l3', title:'Message and Vocabulary', html:'<h3>Message</h3><p>A mother <strong>sacrifices her comfort</strong> raising her children; in old age she deserves <strong>respect, love and care</strong> in return. True beauty is not only youthful looks — it is <strong>wisdom, patience and holiness</strong>.</p><h3>Vocabulary</h3><ul><li><strong>Radiance</strong> — glowing light; <strong>holy</strong> — sacred; <strong>ruined</strong> — destroyed; <strong>still</strong> — calm and quiet; <strong>enlighten</strong> — to give light or knowledge.</li></ul>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'“The Old Woman” was written by:', options:['William Wordsworth','Joseph Campbell','Emily Dickinson','Robert Frost'], answer:1, explain:'The Irish poet Joseph Campbell (1879–1944).'},
{q:'The old woman’s face is compared to a white candle because both:', options:['Are white','Give light and guidance','Melt','Are old'], answer:1, explain:'The candle symbolises holy light; her wisdom guides others.'},
{q:'The “winter’s sun” comparison suggests:', options:['Harsh heat','Soft, gentle warmth of old age','Coldness','Darkness'], answer:1, explain:'Winter sun is mild — like the soft beauty of age.'},
{q:'“Water under a ruined mill” pictures her mind as:', options:['Restless','Still, deep and thoughtful','Empty','Stormy'], answer:1, explain:'Still water = calm, reflective wisdom.'},
{q:'These comparisons are examples of:', options:['Metaphor','Simile','Hyperbole','Pun'], answer:1, explain:'They compare using “like”/“as”.'},
{q:'The poem’s message is that old mothers deserve:', options:['To be ignored','Respect, love and care','To work harder','Silence'], answer:1, explain:'After a life of sacrifice, they merit honour in old age.'},
{q:'The poem finds beauty in:', options:['Youth only','An aged, experienced face','Wealth','Palaces'], answer:1, explain:'Campbell finds radiance in the old woman’s face.'},
{q:'“Radiance” means:', options:['Darkness','Glowing light','Noise','Speed'], answer:1, explain:'Radiance is shining brightness.'},
{q:'How many stanzas does the poem have?', options:['Two','Three','Four','Five'], answer:1, explain:'Three stanzas, one per comparison.'},
{q:'The white candle stands in a:', options:['Market','Holy place','Garden','Kitchen'], answer:1, explain:'The poem says “a white candle in a holy place”.'}
]}
]});

/* ============ KP BOARD — CLASS 10 PHYSICS ============ */
window.__saMergeCourse({id:'kpk-class10-physics', board:'kp', title:'Physics', urdu:'طبیعیات', class:'Class 10', icon:'⚛️', desc:'KP Board Class 10 Physics — full syllabus with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch10', title:'Simple Harmonic Motion and Waves', urdu:'سمپل ہارمونک موشن اور لہریں', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch10-l1', title:'Simple Harmonic Motion', html:'<h3>Definition</h3><p>A body executes <strong>simple harmonic motion (SHM)</strong> when its acceleration is directly proportional to its displacement from the mean position and always directed <strong>towards</strong> the mean position:</p><blockquote>a ∝ −x</blockquote><h3>Key Terms</h3><ul><li><strong>Time period (T):</strong> time for one complete vibration (s).</li><li><strong>Frequency (f):</strong> vibrations per second; f = 1/T (Hz).</li><li><strong>Amplitude:</strong> maximum displacement from the mean position.</li></ul><p>Examples: mass on a spring, a swing, a tuning fork.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch10-l2', title:'The Simple Pendulum', html:'<h3>Time Period</h3><p>A small bob on a light string executes SHM for small angles:</p><blockquote>T = 2π√(l/g)</blockquote><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> l = 1 m, g = 10 m/s² → T = 2π√0.1 ≈ 2 s. A <strong>seconds pendulum</strong> (T = 2 s) is about 1 m long.</p><p>T depends only on length and g — <strong>not</strong> on mass or amplitude (for small swings).</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch10-l3', title:'Wave Motion', html:'<h3>What is a Wave?</h3><p>A wave transfers <strong>energy without transferring matter</strong>.</p><h3>Types</h3><ul><li><strong>Mechanical</strong> (need a medium): sound, water waves. <strong>Electromagnetic</strong> (no medium): light, radio.</li><li><strong>Transverse:</strong> particles vibrate perpendicular to travel (light, water ripples) — have crests and troughs.</li><li><strong>Longitudinal:</strong> particles vibrate parallel to travel (sound) — have compressions and rarefactions.</li></ul><blockquote>v = fλ</blockquote><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> f = 500 Hz, λ = 0.68 m → v = 340 m/s (sound in air).</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'SHM requires acceleration to be:', options:['Constant','Proportional to displacement, towards the mean position','Proportional to velocity','Zero everywhere'], answer:1, explain:'a ∝ −x: proportional to displacement and always towards the mean.'},
{q:'The relation between frequency and time period is:', options:['f = T','f = 1/T','f = T²','f = 2T'], answer:1, explain:'Frequency is vibrations per second, so f = 1/T.'},
{q:'A seconds pendulum has time period:', options:['1 s','2 s','0.5 s','4 s'], answer:1, explain:'One second each way gives T = 2 s.'},
{q:'Time period of a simple pendulum depends on:', options:['Mass of bob','Amplitude','Length and g','Colour of string'], answer:2, explain:'T = 2π√(l/g) — only length and gravity matter.'},
{q:'Which wave needs no medium?', options:['Sound','Water wave','Light','Seismic wave'], answer:2, explain:'Light is electromagnetic and travels through vacuum.'},
{q:'In a transverse wave, particles vibrate:', options:['Parallel to wave travel','Perpendicular to wave travel','In circles only','Not at all'], answer:1, explain:'Perpendicular vibration gives crests and troughs.'},
{q:'Sound waves are:', options:['Transverse','Longitudinal','Electromagnetic','Stationary'], answer:1, explain:'Air particles vibrate parallel to travel — compressions and rarefactions.'},
{q:'A wave with f = 500 Hz and λ = 0.68 m has speed:', options:['340 m/s','740 m/s','170 m/s','500 m/s'], answer:0, explain:'v = fλ = 500 × 0.68 = 340 m/s.'},
{q:'The maximum displacement from the mean position is the:', options:['Wavelength','Amplitude','Frequency','Time period'], answer:1, explain:'Amplitude measures the extreme displacement.'},
{q:'A pendulum of length 0.25 m (g = 10 m/s²) has T ≈:', options:['0.5 s','1 s','2 s','3.14 s'], answer:1, explain:'T = 2π√(0.25/10) = 2π × 0.158 ≈ 1 s.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch11', title:'Sound', urdu:'آواز', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch11-l1', title:'Production and Propagation of Sound', html:'<h3>Production</h3><p>Sound is produced by <strong>vibrating bodies</strong> — vocal cords, guitar strings, a drum’s skin, a tuning fork.</p><h3>Propagation</h3><p>Sound is a <strong>longitudinal mechanical wave</strong> — it <strong>needs a material medium</strong> and cannot travel through vacuum. Speed in air ≈ <strong>343 m/s</strong> (faster in water ~1480 m/s, fastest in solids ~5000 m/s in steel).</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch11-l2', title:'Characteristics of Sound', html:'<h3>Three Qualities</h3><ul><li><strong>Loudness:</strong> depends on <strong>amplitude</strong>; measured in <strong>decibels (dB)</strong>. Intensity ∝ (amplitude)².</li><li><strong>Pitch:</strong> depends on <strong>frequency</strong> — shrill = high pitch, grave = low pitch.</li><li><strong>Quality (timbre):</strong> depends on <strong>waveform</strong> — lets you tell a flute from a violin playing the same note.</li></ul><h3>Audible Range</h3><p>Humans hear <strong>20 Hz – 20,000 Hz</strong>. Below 20 Hz: <strong>infrasound</strong>; above 20 kHz: <strong>ultrasound</strong> (used in medical scans, SONAR).</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch11-l3', title:'Echo, Reverberation and Noise', html:'<h3>Echo</h3><p>An <strong>echo</strong> is sound reflected from a distant surface. The human ear distinguishes it only if it arrives <strong>0.1 s</strong> after the original — needing a distance of at least <strong>17 m</strong>. Bats and SONAR use echoes to navigate.</p><h3>Reverberation</h3><p>Multiple rapid reflections prolong sound (<strong>reverberation</strong>) — good in concert halls (in limits), bad in classrooms. Soft materials (curtains, carpets) absorb sound.</p><h3>Noise Pollution</h3><p>Unwanted loud sound harms hearing and health; silencers, green belts and laws control it.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'Sound is produced by:', options:['Still objects','Vibrating bodies','Light','Magnets'], answer:1, explain:'Vibration is the source of all sound.'},
{q:'Sound cannot travel through:', options:['Air','Water','Vacuum','Steel'], answer:2, explain:'As a mechanical wave it needs a material medium.'},
{q:'The speed of sound in air is about:', options:['3 × 10⁸ m/s','343 m/s','1500 m/s','20 m/s'], answer:1, explain:'≈ 343 m/s at room temperature.'},
{q:'Loudness of sound depends on its:', options:['Frequency','Amplitude','Wavelength','Speed'], answer:1, explain:'Bigger vibrations carry more energy — louder sound.'},
{q:'Pitch depends on:', options:['Amplitude','Frequency','Waveform','Medium'], answer:1, explain:'High frequency = shrill pitch; low frequency = grave.'},
{q:'The audible range for humans is:', options:['0–20 Hz','20 Hz–20 kHz','20 kHz–20 MHz','0–343 Hz'], answer:1, explain:'Below 20 Hz is infrasound; above 20 kHz is ultrasound.'},
{q:'Ultrasound is used in:', options:['Radio broadcast','Medical scans and SONAR','Lighting','Cooking'], answer:1, explain:'High-frequency waves image the body and detect submarines.'},
{q:'An echo is heard distinctly if the reflecting surface is at least:', options:['1.7 m away','17 m away','170 m away','1.7 km away'], answer:1, explain:'Sound must take ≥ 0.1 s for the round trip: 343 × 0.1 / 2 ≈ 17 m.'},
{q:'Timbre (quality) of sound depends on:', options:['Amplitude','Frequency','Waveform','Loudness'], answer:2, explain:'Waveform distinguishes instruments playing the same note.'},
{q:'Reverberation is reduced by:', options:['Polished walls','Soft absorbing materials','Larger halls','Louder speakers'], answer:1, explain:'Curtains and carpets absorb sound instead of reflecting it.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch12', title:'Geometrical Optics', urdu:'جیومیٹریکل آپٹکس', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch12-l1', title:'Reflection of Light', html:'<h3>Laws of Reflection</h3><ul><li>The incident ray, reflected ray and normal all lie in <strong>one plane</strong>.</li><li><strong>Angle of incidence = angle of reflection.</strong></li></ul><h3>Spherical Mirrors</h3><ul><li><strong>Concave:</strong> converging — used in headlights, shaving mirrors, solar furnaces.</li><li><strong>Convex:</strong> diverging — rear-view mirrors (wide field of view), always forms diminished virtual images.</li></ul><blockquote>Mirror formula: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u</blockquote><p>For mirrors, f = R/2.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch12-l2', title:'Refraction of Light', html:'<h3>Laws of Refraction</h3><ul><li>Incident ray, refracted ray and normal lie in one plane.</li><li><strong>Snell’s law:</strong> sin i / sin r = constant = <strong>n</strong> (refractive index).</li></ul><p>Light bends <strong>towards</strong> the normal entering a denser medium. <strong>n = speed in vacuum ÷ speed in medium</strong>; for glass n ≈ 1.5.</p><h3>Total Internal Reflection</h3><p>When light goes from denser to rarer medium beyond the <strong>critical angle</strong>, it reflects fully inside. Uses: <strong>optical fibres</strong>, sparkling diamonds, mirages.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch12-l3', title:'Lenses and the Human Eye', html:'<h3>Lenses</h3><ul><li><strong>Convex (converging):</strong> magnifying glass, camera lens.</li><li><strong>Concave (diverging):</strong> corrects myopia.</li></ul><blockquote>Lens formula: 1/f = 1/v − 1/u; Power P = 1/f (dioptre, D)</blockquote><h3>The Eye</h3><p>The eye’s convex lens focuses images on the <strong>retina</strong>. <strong>Myopia</strong> (short sight): image forms <em>before</em> the retina — corrected with a <strong>concave</strong> lens. <strong>Hyperopia</strong> (long sight): image forms <em>behind</em> — corrected with a <strong>convex</strong> lens.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'The second law of reflection states:', options:['i > r','i = r','i < r','i + r = 90°'], answer:1, explain:'Angle of incidence equals angle of reflection.'},
{q:'A convex mirror is used as a rear-view mirror because it gives:', options:['Magnified image','Wide field of view','Real image','No image'], answer:1, explain:'Diverging mirrors show a wide, diminished, erect image.'},
{q:'In the mirror formula, f equals:', options:['R','R/2','2R','R²'], answer:1, explain:'Focal length is half the radius of curvature.'},
{q:'Snell’s law is:', options:['sin i = sin r','sin i / sin r = n','n = sin r / sin i','i + r = n'], answer:1, explain:'The ratio of sines is the constant refractive index.'},
{q:'Light entering glass from air bends:', options:['Away from the normal','Towards the normal','Along the surface','Backwards'], answer:1, explain:'Denser medium slows light, bending it towards the normal.'},
{q:'Total internal reflection needs light going from:', options:['Rarer to denser','Denser to rarer beyond the critical angle','Air to vacuum','Any two media'], answer:1, explain:'Both conditions — denser→rarer and i > critical angle — are required.'},
{q:'Optical fibres work on the principle of:', options:['Refraction','Total internal reflection','Diffraction','Polarisation'], answer:1, explain:'Light stays trapped inside the fibre by repeated total internal reflection.'},
{q:'Power of a lens of focal length 0.5 m is:', options:['0.5 D','2 D','5 D','50 D'], answer:1, explain:'P = 1/f = 1/0.5 = 2 dioptres.'},
{q:'Myopia is corrected with a:', options:['Convex lens','Concave lens','Plane mirror','Prism'], answer:1, explain:'A diverging lens pushes the image back onto the retina.'},
{q:'In hyperopia, the image forms:', options:['On the retina','Behind the retina','In front of the retina','On the lens'], answer:1, explain:'The eyeball is too short; a convex lens adds convergence.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch13', title:'Electrostatics', urdu:'الیکٹروسٹیٹکس', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch13-l1', title:'Electric Charge', html:'<h3>Charges</h3><p>There are two kinds: <strong>positive</strong> and <strong>negative</strong>. <strong>Like charges repel; unlike charges attract.</strong> Charge is measured in <strong>coulombs (C)</strong>; the electron’s charge is −1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.</p><h3>Charging</h3><ul><li><strong>By rubbing (friction):</strong> glass rod → positive, ebonite → negative.</li><li><strong>By induction:</strong> a charged body attracts opposite charges in a neutral object without touching.</li></ul><p>An <strong>electroscope</strong> detects charge: its leaves diverge when charged.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch13-l2', title:'Coulomb’s Law and Electric Field', html:'<h3>Coulomb’s Law</h3><blockquote>F = k·Qq / r²,  k = 9 × 10⁹ N·m²/C²</blockquote><p>Force is proportional to the charges and inversely proportional to the square of their distance.</p><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> two 1 C charges 1 m apart: F = 9 × 10⁹ N — enormous, showing the coulomb is a huge unit.</p><h3>Electric Field</h3><p>The <strong>electric field</strong> is the region where a charge feels force. <strong>Field lines</strong> go <em>out</em> of positive and <em>into</em> negative charges; they never cross.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch13-l3', title:'Potential and Capacitors', html:'<h3>Electric Potential</h3><p><strong>Potential (V)</strong> = work done per unit charge bringing it from infinity: V = W/Q. Unit: <strong>volt (V)</strong>. The Earth is taken as zero potential.</p><h3>Capacitors</h3><p>A <strong>capacitor</strong> stores charge. <strong>Capacitance:</strong></p><blockquote>C = Q/V (unit: farad, F)</blockquote><p>Capacitance grows with plate area and shrinks with plate separation. Uses: camera flash, filters, tuning radios. <strong>Hazards:</strong> lightning, sparking at petrol pumps — earthing and lightning conductors protect us.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'The SI unit of electric charge is the:', options:['Volt','Coulomb','Ampere','Farad'], answer:1, explain:'Charge is measured in coulombs (C).'},
{q:'Like charges:', options:['Attract','Repel','Have no effect','Combine'], answer:1, explain:'Like repels like; opposites attract.'},
{q:'An electroscope is used to:', options:['Measure current','Detect electric charge','Store charge','Measure voltage'], answer:1, explain:'Its gold leaves diverge when the cap is charged.'},
{q:'Coulomb’s law: F is proportional to:', options:['r²','1/r²','r','√r'], answer:1, explain:'F = kQq/r² — inverse-square law like gravity.'},
{q:'The value of k in Coulomb’s law is:', options:['6.67 × 10⁻¹¹','9 × 10⁹ N·m²/C²','1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹','3 × 10⁸'], answer:1, explain:'k = 9 × 10⁹ N·m²/C².'},
{q:'Electric field lines around a positive charge point:', options:['Inwards','Outwards','In circles','Randomly'], answer:1, explain:'Lines leave positive charges and enter negative ones.'},
{q:'The volt is the unit of:', options:['Charge','Electric potential','Capacitance','Field'], answer:1, explain:'Potential = work per unit charge, in volts.'},
{q:'Capacitance is defined as:', options:['Q × V','Q/V','V/Q','Q²/V'], answer:1, explain:'C = Q/V, in farads.'},
{q:'Capacitance increases when the plate area:', options:['Decreases','Increases','Is halved','Becomes zero'], answer:1, explain:'Larger plates hold more charge at the same voltage.'},
{q:'Lightning conductors protect buildings by:', options:['Attracting lightning away','Providing an easy earth path for the charge','Blocking clouds','Insulating the roof'], answer:1, explain:'A thick copper strip carries the strike safely to earth.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch14', title:'Current Electricity', urdu:'کرنٹ الیکٹرسٹی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch14-l1', title:'Current, Voltage and Resistance', html:'<h3>Current</h3><blockquote>I = Q/t (unit: ampere, A)</blockquote><p>1 A = 1 C/s. <strong>Conventional current</strong> flows + to −; electrons actually flow − to +.</p><h3>Ohm’s Law</h3><blockquote>V = IR</blockquote><p>Current through a conductor is directly proportional to the voltage across it (at constant temperature). Unit of resistance: <strong>ohm (Ω)</strong>.</p><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> 12 V across 4 Ω → I = 3 A.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch14-l2', title:'Factors and Combinations', html:'<h3>Factors of Resistance</h3><p>R = ρL/A: resistance grows with <strong>length</strong>, shrinks with <strong>area</strong>, and depends on material (<strong>resistivity ρ</strong>) and temperature.</p><h3>Combinations</h3><ul><li><strong>Series:</strong> R = R₁ + R₂ + … (same current; voltage divides). Used in fuses and decoration lights.</li><li><strong>Parallel:</strong> 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + … (same voltage; current divides). Used in house wiring — one bulb failing does not kill the rest.</li></ul><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> 2 Ω and 3 Ω in parallel: 1/R = 1/2 + 1/3 = 5/6 → R = 1.2 Ω.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch14-l3', title:'Electric Power and Safety', html:'<h3>Power</h3><blockquote>P = VI = I²R = V²/R (unit: watt, W)</blockquote><p>Energy: E = Pt. The billing unit is the <strong>kilowatt-hour</strong>: 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J.</p><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> a 100 W bulb for 10 h uses 1 kWh (one “unit”).</p><h3>Safety</h3><p><strong>Fuses</strong> and <strong>circuit breakers</strong> melt/break on overload. <strong>Earthing</strong> carries fault current to ground. Never touch switches with wet hands.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'One ampere equals:', options:['1 J/s','1 C/s','1 V/s','1 Ω/s'], answer:1, explain:'I = Q/t, so 1 A = 1 coulomb per second.'},
{q:'Ohm’s law is stated as:', options:['V = I/R','V = IR','I = VR','R = VI'], answer:1, explain:'Voltage = current × resistance.'},
{q:'12 V across 4 Ω gives current:', options:['48 A','3 A','8 A','16 A'], answer:1, explain:'I = V/R = 12/4 = 3 A.'},
{q:'Resistance of a wire increases when its:', options:['Area increases','Length increases','Temperature drops to zero','Material changes to copper'], answer:1, explain:'R = ρL/A — longer wire, more resistance.'},
{q:'Two resistors 2 Ω and 3 Ω in series give:', options:['1.2 Ω','5 Ω','6 Ω','0.83 Ω'], answer:1, explain:'Series: R = 2 + 3 = 5 Ω.'},
{q:'House wiring uses parallel circuits so that:', options:['Voltage divides','Each appliance gets full voltage and works independently','Current is same everywhere','Less wire is used'], answer:1, explain:'Parallel gives every appliance 220 V; one failing does not stop others.'},
{q:'Electric power can be written as:', options:['V/I','VI','V²I','I/V'], answer:1, explain:'P = VI = I²R = V²/R.'},
{q:'One kilowatt-hour equals:', options:['3600 J','3.6 × 10⁶ J','1000 J','60 J'], answer:1, explain:'1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 MJ.'},
{q:'A 100 W bulb used for 10 hours consumes:', options:['10 kWh','1 kWh','100 kWh','0.1 kWh'], answer:1, explain:'0.1 kW × 10 h = 1 kWh (one unit).'},
{q:'A fuse protects a circuit by:', options:['Increasing voltage','Melting and breaking on overload','Storing charge','Reducing resistance'], answer:1, explain:'Its thin wire melts when current exceeds the rating.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch15', title:'Electromagnetism', urdu:'الیکٹرومیگنیٹزم', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch15-l1', title:'Magnetic Effect of Current', html:'<h3>Oersted’s Discovery</h3><p>A current-carrying wire produces a <strong>magnetic field</strong> around it (compass needle deflects). Field direction follows the <strong>right-hand grip rule</strong>.</p><h3>Electromagnet</h3><p>A soft-iron core inside a solenoid becomes a strong <strong>temporary magnet</strong> — used in cranes, electric bells and relays. Strength rises with more turns and more current.</p><h3>Force on a Conductor</h3><blockquote>F = BIL</blockquote><p>Direction by <strong>Fleming’s left-hand rule</strong> (thumb = force, forefinger = field, middle finger = current). Basis of the <strong>DC motor</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch15-l2', title:'Electromagnetic Induction', html:'<h3>Faraday’s Law</h3><p><em>A changing magnetic field induces an EMF in a coil</em> — faster change or more turns give bigger EMF. <strong>Lenz’s law:</strong> the induced current <strong>opposes</strong> the change producing it.</p><h3>AC Generator</h3><p>Rotating a coil in a magnetic field induces <strong>alternating current</strong>. Direction found by <strong>Fleming’s right-hand rule</strong>. Pakistan’s mains supply is <strong>220 V, 50 Hz</strong>.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch15-l3', title:'Transformers', html:'<h3>Working</h3><p>A <strong>transformer</strong> changes AC voltage using mutual induction between primary and secondary coils on an iron core.</p><blockquote>Vs/Vp = Ns/Np</blockquote><ul><li><strong>Step-up:</strong> Ns &gt; Np (raises voltage for transmission).</li><li><strong>Step-down:</strong> Ns &lt; Np (e.g. 220 V → 12 V for chargers).</li></ul><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> 220 V primary, 440 secondary turns vs 220 primary turns → Vs = 440 V. Transformers work on <strong>AC only</strong>.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'Who discovered the magnetic effect of current?', options:['Faraday','Oersted','Ampere','Maxwell'], answer:1, explain:'Oersted (1820) saw a compass deflect near a current wire.'},
{q:'An electromagnet uses a:', options:['Steel core (permanent)','Soft-iron core (temporary)','Copper core','Wooden core'], answer:1, explain:'Soft iron magnetises and demagnetises easily.'},
{q:'Fleming’s left-hand rule gives the direction of:', options:['Induced current','Force on a current-carrying conductor','Magnetic field','EMF'], answer:1, explain:'Thumb = motion/force — the motor rule.'},
{q:'Faraday’s law states that induced EMF depends on the:', options:['Strength of magnet only','Rate of change of magnetic flux','Colour of wire','Room temperature'], answer:1, explain:'Faster flux change → larger EMF.'},
{q:'Lenz’s law says the induced current:', options:['Supports the change','Opposes the change producing it','Is always zero','Flows randomly'], answer:1, explain:'Opposition to change — conservation of energy.'},
{q:'Pakistan’s mains electricity is:', options:['110 V, 60 Hz','220 V, 50 Hz','440 V, 50 Hz','220 V, 60 Hz'], answer:1, explain:'Standard domestic supply: 220 V alternating at 50 Hz.'},
{q:'A transformer with Ns > Np is:', options:['Step-down','Step-up','Isolating only','DC converter'], answer:1, explain:'More secondary turns → higher secondary voltage.'},
{q:'If Vp = 220 V, Np = 100, Ns = 50, then Vs is:', options:['440 V','110 V','220 V','55 V'], answer:1, explain:'Vs = Vp × Ns/Np = 220 × 0.5 = 110 V.'},
{q:'Transformers work on:', options:['DC only','AC only','Both AC and DC','Batteries'], answer:1, explain:'They need a changing flux, which only AC provides.'},
{q:'High voltage is used in power transmission to:', options:['Increase power','Reduce current and energy loss','Make wires glow','Charge batteries'], answer:1, explain:'P = VI: high V means low I, so I²R losses shrink.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch16', title:'Basic Electronics', urdu:'بنیادی الیکٹرانکس', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch16-l1', title:'Thermionic Emission and Cathode Rays', html:'<h3>Thermionic Emission</h3><p>Heating a metal <strong>cathode</strong> boils <strong>electrons</strong> off its surface. In an <strong>electron gun</strong>, these electrons are accelerated towards an <strong>anode</strong>.</p><h3>Cathode Rays</h3><p>Streams of fast electrons — they travel straight, carry energy and glow on fluorescent screens. Used in old TV tubes.</p><h3>CRO</h3><p>The <strong>cathode ray oscilloscope</strong> displays voltage waveforms — used to study sound, AC mains and heartbeats (ECG).</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch16-l2', title:'Analogue vs Digital', html:'<h3>Two Worlds</h3><ul><li><strong>Analogue:</strong> continuous values (a mercury thermometer, vinyl record).</li><li><strong>Digital:</strong> only two states — <strong>0 and 1 (bits)</strong>.</li></ul><p>Digital signals resist noise and are easy to store — why computers, CDs and mobile phones are digital.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch16-l3', title:'Logic Gates', html:'<h3>Basic Gates</h3><table><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Output is 1 when</th></tr><tr><td>AND</td><td><strong>all</strong> inputs are 1</td></tr><tr><td>OR</td><td><strong>any</strong> input is 1</td></tr><tr><td>NOT</td><td>input is 0 (inverts)</td></tr><tr><td>NAND</td><td>NOT(AND) — 0 only if all inputs 1</td></tr><tr><td>NOR</td><td>NOT(OR) — 1 only if all inputs 0</td></tr></table><p>Inputs A=1, B=0: AND→0, OR→1, NAND→1, NOR→0. Gates are the building blocks of all computers.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'Thermionic emission is the release of ___ from a heated metal.', options:['Protons','Electrons','Neutrons','Photons'], answer:1, explain:'Heat gives electrons enough energy to escape the cathode.'},
{q:'In an electron gun, electrons are accelerated towards the:', options:['Cathode','Anode','Grid','Screen'], answer:1, explain:'The positive anode attracts the negative electrons.'},
{q:'A CRO is used to:', options:['Measure mass','Display voltage waveforms','Weigh chemicals','Cool circuits'], answer:1, explain:'The cathode ray oscilloscope draws electrical signals on screen.'},
{q:'Digital signals use:', options:['Continuous values','Only 0 and 1','Infinite levels','Sound waves'], answer:1, explain:'Binary digits (bits) are the language of computers.'},
{q:'An AND gate gives 1 when:', options:['Any input is 1','All inputs are 1','No input is 1','Inputs differ'], answer:1, explain:'AND needs every input high.'},
{q:'A NOT gate with input 1 gives output:', options:['1','0','2','Undefined'], answer:1, explain:'NOT inverts: 1 → 0.'},
{q:'For inputs A=1, B=0, an OR gate gives:', options:['0','1','2','10'], answer:1, explain:'OR is 1 if any input is 1.'},
{q:'NAND is the combination of:', options:['NOT + OR','AND + NOT','OR + AND','NOT + NOT'], answer:1, explain:'NAND = NOT(AND): invert the AND output.'},
{q:'For inputs A=0, B=0, a NOR gate gives:', options:['0','1','2','Undefined'], answer:1, explain:'NOR is 1 only when all inputs are 0.'},
{q:'Logic gates are the building blocks of:', options:['Bridges','Computers','Dams','Engines'], answer:1, explain:'Billions of gates form processors and memory.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch17', title:'Information and Communication Technology', urdu:'انفارمیشن اینڈ کمیونیکیشن ٹیکنالوجی', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch17-l1', title:'What is ICT?', html:'<h3>Definition</h3><p><strong>ICT</strong> is the technology for <strong>creating, storing, processing and communicating information</strong> — computers, the internet, mobile phones, radio and TV.</p><h3>Components</h3><ul><li><strong>Hardware:</strong> physical parts (CPU, monitor, keyboard).</li><li><strong>Software:</strong> programs and operating systems.</li><li><strong>Networks:</strong> LAN (local), WAN (wide, e.g. the internet).</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch17-l2', title:'Telecommunication', html:'<h3>Tools</h3><ul><li><strong>Telephone/mobile:</strong> voice calls and SMS.</li><li><strong>Fax:</strong> sends document images over phone lines.</li><li><strong>Internet:</strong> emails, video calls, e-learning, e-commerce.</li><li><strong>Radio/TV:</strong> broadcast mass communication.</li></ul><p><strong>Modem</strong> converts digital computer data to analogue signals for transmission and back.</p>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch17-l3', title:'Uses and Impact', html:'<h3>Uses</h3><ul><li><strong>Education:</strong> online classes and digital libraries.</li><li><strong>Health:</strong> telemedicine, patient records.</li><li><strong>Business:</strong> e-banking, online shopping.</li><li><strong>Government:</strong> NADRA records, online services.</li></ul><h3>Care</h3><p>Protect privacy, avoid <strong>plagiarism</strong> and misinformation, limit screen time, and keep software updated against viruses.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'ICT stands for:', options:['International Computer Technology','Information and Communication Technology','Internet Cable Transmission','Integrated Circuit Theory'], answer:1, explain:'Technologies for handling and communicating information.'},
{q:'Which of these is hardware?', options:['Windows','MS Word','Keyboard','Antivirus'], answer:2, explain:'Hardware is the physical part; the rest are software.'},
{q:'A LAN is a network covering a:', options:['Country','Small local area','Whole planet','City only'], answer:1, explain:'Local Area Network: a building or campus.'},
{q:'A modem converts:', options:['AC to DC','Digital to analogue and back','Sound to light','Heat to power'], answer:1, explain:'Modulate/demodulate bridges computers and phone lines.'},
{q:'E-banking is a use of ICT in:', options:['Education','Business','Health','Sports'], answer:1, explain:'Online banking is a business/finance application.'},
{q:'NADRA uses ICT mainly for:', options:['Games','Citizen records and identity','Movies','Music'], answer:1, explain:'Digital national identity records.'},
{q:'Using another’s work as your own is:', options:['Research','Plagiarism','Browsing','Typing'], answer:1, explain:'Plagiarism is academic dishonesty.'},
{q:'Telemedicine brings ICT to:', options:['Farming','Healthcare','Cinema','Travel'], answer:1, explain:'Remote diagnosis and consultation.'},
{q:'Which is an output device?', options:['Keyboard','Mouse','Monitor','Scanner'], answer:2, explain:'The monitor displays results; the rest are inputs.'},
{q:'The internet is an example of a:', options:['LAN','WAN','PAN','Cable'], answer:1, explain:'A Wide Area Network spanning the globe.'}
]},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch18', title:'Atomic and Nuclear Physics', urdu:'ایٹمی اور نیوکلیئر فزکس', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch18-l1', title:'Radioactivity', html:'<h3>Discovery</h3><p><strong>Becquerel</strong> (1896) discovered <strong>radioactivity</strong> — the spontaneous decay of unstable nuclei, studied by Marie and Pierre <strong>Curie</strong>.</p><h3>The Three Radiations</h3><table><tr><th></th><th>Alpha (α)</th><th>Beta (β)</th><th>Gamma (γ)</th></tr><tr><td>Nature</td><td>Helium nuclei</td><td>Electrons</td><td>EM waves</td></tr><tr><td>Penetration</td><td>Stopped by paper</td><td>Stopped by aluminium</td><td>Needs lead</td></tr><tr><td>Ionisation</td><td>Highest</td><td>Medium</td><td>Lowest</td></tr></table>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch18-l2', title:'Half-Life and Nuclear Reactions', html:'<h3>Half-Life</h3><p><strong>Half-life</strong> is the time for half the nuclei to decay. After n half-lives, (1/2)ⁿ remains. <strong>Worked example:</strong> 80 g with 10-year half-life → after 30 years: 80 → 40 → 20 → <strong>10 g</strong>.</p><h3>Fission and Fusion</h3><ul><li><strong>Fission:</strong> a heavy nucleus (U-235) splits into lighter ones + huge energy — nuclear reactors and bombs.</li><li><strong>Fusion:</strong> light nuclei (hydrogen) join — powers the <strong>Sun</strong>; E = mc².</li></ul>'},
{id:'kpk-c10-phy-ch18-l3', title:'Uses and Hazards', html:'<h3>Peaceful Uses</h3><ul><li><strong>Power:</strong> nuclear plants (e.g. Karachi, Chashma) generate electricity.</li><li><strong>Medicine:</strong> I-131 treats thyroid; Co-60 treats cancer.</li><li><strong>Agriculture/industry:</strong> tracers, pest control, thickness gauges.</li></ul><h3>Hazards and Safety</h3><p>Radiation damages cells and DNA. Safety: <strong>time</strong> (minimise exposure), <strong>distance</strong>, <strong>shielding</strong> (lead), and strict waste disposal.</p>'}],
quiz:[
{q:'Radioactivity was discovered by:', options:['Marie Curie','Becquerel','Rutherford','Einstein'], answer:1, explain:'Becquerel (1896) found uranium fogging photographic plates.'},
{q:'Alpha particles are:', options:['Electrons','Helium nuclei','EM waves','Protons'], answer:1, explain:'α = 2 protons + 2 neutrons.'},
{q:'Which radiation is stopped by a sheet of paper?', options:['Beta','Gamma','Alpha','X-rays'], answer:2, explain:'Heavy, highly ionising alpha particles penetrate least.'},
{q:'Gamma rays are stopped by:', options:['Paper','Aluminium','Thick lead','Wood'], answer:2, explain:'Only dense lead blocks penetrating gamma rays.'},
{q:'Half-life is the time for:', options:['All nuclei to decay','Half the nuclei to decay','Atoms to double','Electrons to escape'], answer:1, explain:'Each half-life halves the remaining amount.'},
{q:'80 g of a substance with 10-year half-life remains after 30 years:', options:['40 g','20 g','10 g','5 g'], answer:2, explain:'Three half-lives: 80 → 40 → 20 → 10 g.'},
{q:'Nuclear fission is the:', options:['Joining of light nuclei','Splitting of a heavy nucleus','Decay of electrons','Burning of coal'], answer:1, explain:'U-235 splits into lighter nuclei releasing energy.'},
{q:'The Sun’s energy comes from:', options:['Fission','Fusion','Combustion','Friction'], answer:1, explain:'Hydrogen fuses into helium in the Sun’s core.'},
{q:'Co-60 is used in medicine for:', options:['X-ray imaging','Cancer treatment','Vaccines','Surgery'], answer:1, explain:'Its gamma rays destroy cancer cells (radiotherapy).'},
{q:'E = mc² shows that:', options:['Energy equals mass always','Mass can convert into enormous energy','Light is slow','Atoms are indivisible'], answer:1, explain:'Tiny mass × c² gives huge energy — the basis of nuclear power.'}
]}
]});

